// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ── Chinese ──
  "chinese:hunt": "Every autumn from 1681 the Kangxi Emperor (康熙帝) rode north beyond the Great Wall (长城) to the Mulan hunting grounds (木兰围场). Thousands of bannermen formed a vast ring through the forest and slowly drew it tight, driving deer, boar and tigers toward the emperor's arrows. It was sport, but also drill: the Manchu court feared that peace and city life would soften its riders. Kangxi was proud of his tally of tigers, and the hunt ended with feasts for the Mongol princes who came to watch.",
  "chinese:battle": "In 1561 the coast of Zhejiang (浙江) burned under raids by the wokou pirates (倭寇). General Qi Jiguang (戚继光) had recruited miners and farmers from Yiwu (义乌) and drilled them in the mandarin-duck formation (鸳鸯阵): twelve men moving as one, rattan shields in front, long bamboo spears with their branches left on to tangle the enemy's swords. At Taizhou (台州) they won nine battles in a row. For the villages along the shore it felt like the first time in years the sea could be watched without dread.",
  "chinese:ritual": "At the winter solstice the Ming (明) and Qing (清) emperors left the Forbidden City (紫禁城) for the Temple of Heaven (天坛), built under Yongle (永乐) in 1420. Before dawn, in the cold, the Son of Heaven (天子) climbed the three white marble tiers of the Circular Mound Altar (圜丘坛) to offer jade, silk, a burnt calf and music to Heaven. Musicians played the slow, ancient court music (雅乐). The whole realm's harvest and peace for the coming year was felt to rest on this one man bowing correctly in the dark.",
  "chinese:rain": "From 1876 to 1879 the rain failed across northern China: the Dingwu Famine (丁戊奇荒), when perhaps nine to thirteen million people died. Magistrates led processions to the dragon king's temple, set his image out in the sun so he would feel the drought, and carried willow branches. Farmers watched the cracked fields and the empty sky every morning. When the rains at last returned, the people who had survived planted again, quietly, because that is what one does.",
  "chinese:wind": "Between 1405 and 1433 Admiral Zheng He (郑和) led seven great treasure fleets across the Indian Ocean. At Liujiagang (刘家港) and Changle (长乐) he set up stone tablets thanking Tianfei (天妃), the sea goddess Mazu (妈祖), for saving them in storms: when the wind howled and the waves stood like mountains, the sailors cried out to her and a holy light appeared at the masthead, and the sea grew calm. The tablets read less like boasts than like the relieved prayers of men who had expected to drown.",
  "chinese-early:fire": "In 206 BC, after the fall of Qin (秦), the rebel lord Xiang Yu (项羽) entered Xianyang (咸阳) and set fire to the palaces of the First Emperor. Sima Qian (司马迁) wrote that the fire burned for three months. The legendary Epang Palace (阿房宫) became a byword: centuries later the poet Du Mu (杜牧) wrote that the people of Chu (楚) lit one torch and it all turned to scorched earth, and warned that rulers who do not pity their people will in turn be pitied by those who come after.",
  "chinese:thunder": "In Chinese villages thunder was the voice of Leigong (雷公), the Duke of Thunder, with a bird's beak, wings and a hammer and chisel, and of his partner Dianmu (电母), the Mother of Lightning, whose mirror lit up the wicked so his strike would not miss. People said that one who was unfilial or wasted rice would be struck by heaven's thunder (天打雷劈). When a storm rolled over, children were told to finish their bowls and be kind to their grandparents.",
  "chinese:birth": "After a birth a mother of the Ming (明) and Qing (清) kept the month of confinement (坐月子), shut away from wind and cold water and fed chicken soup with ginger. On the child's first birthday came the zhuazhou (抓周): brush, coins, a seal, an abacus and a toy sword were set on a tray, and the family leaned in to see what the baby grabbed. In Cao Xueqin's (曹雪芹) Dream of the Red Chamber (红楼梦), written in the 1750s, little Jia Baoyu (贾宝玉) ignored them all and seized rouge and hairpins, and his stern father decided he would never amount to much. Readers laughed, and families went on watching just as closely.",
  "chinese:death": "When a parent died, a Chinese family wore undyed hemp (披麻戴孝) and wailed by the coffin. A Daoist or Buddhist priest chanted, paper money and paper houses were burned so the dead would not be poor in the other world, and on the seventh day (头七) the soul was thought to visit home one last time. Under Confucian rules a son mourned a parent for three years, leaving office if he held one. Grief was not hidden; it was a duty carried out with care.",

  // ── Shang ──
  "shang:hunt": "The Shang (商) kings hunted constantly, and they asked the ancestors first. On ox shoulder blades and turtle shells (甲骨) the diviners carved questions: will the king hunt at this place today without disaster? Afterward they sometimes carved the result: so many deer, so many boar. One famous bone, from the last reign, records that the king caught a great wild rhinoceros (兕) and was given a gift. The hunt was royal pleasure, military practice and a test of whether heaven still favoured the house of Shang.",
  "shang:battle": "Around 1200 BC, oracle bones of King Wu Ding (武丁) record his consort Fu Hao (妇好) raising some thirteen thousand troops against the Qiang (羌) people in the west, the largest army mentioned anywhere in the bones. She led other campaigns too, against the Tu and the Yi. Bronze axes with her name were found in her tomb. The diviners asked again and again whether she would receive divine help. For the Shang (商), war was something the ancestors joined, and a general was also a priest.",
  "shang:gather": "In the loess valleys around Anyang (安阳) the Shang (商) grew millet, and every season the diviners asked the same anxious question: will we receive the harvest (受年)? Laborers called zhongren (众人) worked the fields together under royal officers, cutting the heavy heads of millet with stone and shell sickles. Part of the grain went into ale for the ancestors, who were believed to be hungry too. A good harvest meant the spirits were content, and the king's divinations were being answered well.",
  "shang:ritual": "In the royal temple at Yin (殷) the diviner drilled hollows into a cleaned turtle plastron, then pressed a heated bronze rod into them. The shell cracked with a sharp sound (卜 still looks like those cracks). The king read the lines himself: the king prognosticated, saying (王占曰) it will be auspicious. Questions covered everything: rain, war, harvest, toothache, dreams. Offerings of cattle, wine and sometimes captives were made to Di (帝) and the royal ancestors. It was solemn work: asking the dead to reveal tomorrow.",
  "shang:rain": "One of the best-known oracle bones from Anyang (安阳) asks, on the day guimao: today, will it rain? Will the rain come from the west? From the east? From the north? From the south? Shang (商) life depended on the rains reaching their millet fields, and the diviners carved the question to all four directions as if turning to look at each horizon. Many other bones record the king offering to the river and the mountain spirits, and simply waiting for clouds.",
  "shang:flood": "The Shang (商) moved their capital several times, and later tradition blamed the Yellow River's (黄河) floods. When King Pan Geng (盘庚) moved the court to Yin (殷) around 1300 BC, the people did not want to go. The Book of Documents (尚书) preserves his speeches: he scolded, persuaded, and promised that the ancestors themselves wished the move. The new capital at Anyang (安阳) lasted over two centuries. Behind the speeches one can still hear an ordinary people afraid of leaving their homes for higher ground.",
  "shang:wind": "The Shang (商) believed the winds came from the four quarters, each with its own spirit and name. A famous bone lists them: the east is called Xi and its wind Xie; the south is Yin (殷) and its wind Kai, and so on (四方风). The wind was also thought to be a messenger of the high god Di, sometimes written with the same sign as the phoenix (凤). When a great storm blew, the diviners asked whether it would bring harm to the king.",
  "shang:fire": "In 1046 BC King Wu of Zhou defeated the last Shang (商) king, Di Xin (帝辛), at the battle of Muye (牧野). Sima Qian (司马迁) tells that Di Xin fled back to his capital, climbed the Deer Terrace (鹿台), dressed in his jade-covered robes, and set himself on fire. Six hundred years of Shang rule ended in that blaze. The Zhou later called him a tyrant, but for the people of Shang it was the night their whole world of ancestors and oracles burned.",
  "shang:thunder": "To the Shang (商), rain, wind and thunder were commanded by Di (帝), the high god above the ancestors, and the diviners asked whether Di would order thunder in a given month. The ancestors could be asked to plead for good weather, but Di himself received few direct offerings; he was too high and too unpredictable. When thunder broke over the Anyang (安阳) plain, the Shang heard the will of a power that even their kings could only question, never command.",
  "shang:birth": "The oracle bones preserve a real divination about a royal birth. On day jiashen the king asked: Fu Hao will give birth; will it be good (妇好娩，嘉)? King Wu Ding (武丁) read the cracks and said that if she gave birth on a ding day it would be good. Weeks later the result was carved: it was not good, it was a girl. Fu Hao herself went on to lead armies. The bone shows both the danger of every birth and the hopes a family pressed onto it.",
  "shang:death": "In 1976 archaeologists at Anyang (安阳) opened the untouched tomb of Fu Hao (妇好), who died around 1200 BC. She had been laid in a lacquered coffin with 468 bronzes, 755 jades, cowrie shells, ivory cups, sixteen attendants and six dogs. King Wu Ding (武丁) kept making offerings and asking about her spirit after her death. Shang (商) funerals were grand and frightening: the dead remained powerful ancestors, and they had to be given everything they would need.",

  // ── Shu ──
  "shu:hunt": "Imagined: in the hills above the Chengdu (成都) plain, Shu (蜀) officers rode out with dogs and bows in late autumn, chasing deer through bamboo thickets. The hunt fed the garrison, but it was also practice for the mountain roads to the north, where Zhuge Liang's (诸葛亮) armies would have to march. The men talked of home in Jing province, lost to Wu (吴), and wondered whether the Han dynasty they served would ever return to Chang'an (长安).",
  "shu:battle": "In 219 at Mount Dingjun (定军山), Liu Bei's (刘备) army fought for control of Hanzhong (汉中). Fa Zheng (法正) watched for the moment, and the old general Huang Zhong (黄忠), already past sixty, charged down from the heights with drums roaring. The Wei (魏) commander Xiahou Yuan (夏侯渊) was killed. Liu Bei took Hanzhong and proclaimed himself its king. For the men of Shu (蜀) it proved that their small state, cut off behind the mountains, could still beat the north.",
  "shu:gather": "Shu was famous for its brocade (蜀锦), woven from silk raised along the Jin River (锦江) in Chengdu (成都), where the cloth was washed until its colors shone. Women gathered mulberry leaves at dawn to feed the silkworms. Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) wrote that the state now relied on brocade alone to fund its armies against the enemy. Every basket of leaves from the orchards was, in a small way, part of the war to restore the Han.",
  "shu:ritual": "In the spring of 221, after news came that the last Han emperor had been deposed by Cao Pi (曹丕), Liu Bei (刘备) ascended an altar south of Chengdu (成都) at Wudan and offered a sacrifice to Heaven and Earth. His proclamation declared that the Han had not ended: he would carry on its mandate. For his followers this was a solemn act of faith, asking Heaven to recognize a dynasty that by now held only one mountain-ringed province.",
  "shu:rain": "In 230 the Wei (魏) general Cao Zhen (曹真) marched against Shu (蜀) through the Ziwu (子午) valley. Shu could only fortify the passes and wait. Then the rain began and did not stop for more than thirty days. The plank roads clinging to the cliffs washed away, and the Wei army was ordered home. In Hanzhong (汉中) the defenders, who had braced for a desperate fight, watched the clouds and felt the mountains and the sky had taken their side.",
  "shu:flood": "In the autumn of 219 Guan Yu (关羽) besieged Fancheng (樊城) on the Han River (汉水). Heavy rains swelled the river until it burst its banks, and the seven armies under Yu Jin (于禁), camped on low ground, were swamped (水淹七军). Guan Yu's men came by boat; Yu Jin surrendered, and Pang De (庞德), who refused to, was executed. For a few weeks all of central China trembled. Within months Guan Yu himself would be killed, but the flood remained his greatest moment.",
  "shu:wind": "Before the battle of Red Cliffs (赤壁) in 208, the histories say only that a southeast wind blew, carrying Huang Gai's (黄盖) fire ships into Cao Cao's (曹操) fleet. The later Romance of the Three Kingdoms gives the wind to Shu (蜀): Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) climbs an altar on the south bank, unbinds his hair, and borrows the east wind (借东风) through his arts. The legend shows how the people of Shu wanted to remember their strategist: as a man who could talk to the sky.",
  "shu:fire": "In 222 Liu Bei (刘备) invaded Wu (吴) to avenge Guan Yu (关羽). His army camped in a chain of forts stretching hundreds of li through the forests at Yiling (夷陵) in the summer heat. The young Wu commander Lu Xun (陆逊) waited, then sent men with torches. The camps burned one after another. Liu Bei fled to Baidicheng (白帝城) in shame and never returned to Chengdu (成都). Shu (蜀) would remember that fire as the night it lost a generation of soldiers.",
  "shu:thunder": "While Liu Bei (刘备) was a guest of Cao Cao (曹操), Cao said to him over wine: the only heroes in the world are you and I. Liu Bei was so startled he dropped his chopsticks. Later tellings add that at that very moment thunder cracked, and Liu Bei calmly blamed his fright on it (煮酒论英雄). Whether or not the thunder was real, the story shows how dangerously Liu Bei had to live, hiding his ambition under the storm.",
  "shu:birth": "Liu Shan (刘禅), called A Dou, was born in 207 to Liu Bei (刘备), who was nearly fifty. The next year at Changban (长坂), as Cao Cao's (曹操) cavalry overran the refugees, Zhao Yun (赵云) carried the infant through the chaos to safety. The whole hope of a dynasty rested on that baby. He reigned for forty years, but in 263 he surrendered to Wei (魏), and later said he was too happy in Luoyang (洛阳) to miss Shu (蜀). No one at his birth could have guessed either ending.",
  "shu:death": "Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) died in camp at Wuzhang Plains (五丈原) in 234, during his last campaign. He had asked to be buried simply at Mount Dingjun (定军山) in Hanzhong (汉中), in a tomb only big enough for the coffin, in his ordinary clothes, with no precious objects. The army withdrew in silence. Ordinary people in Shu (蜀) began to offer to him privately in the fields, and in time the court allowed shrines. He is still mourned in Chengdu (成都) today.",

  // ── Wei ──
  "wei:hunt": "Cao Pi (曹丕), first emperor of Wei (魏), loved hunting and wrote about it with pride. Once he rode out with his son Cao Rui (曹叡) and shot a mother deer. He told the boy to shoot the fawn. Cao Rui lowered his bow and wept: Your Majesty has already killed its mother; I cannot bear to kill the child. Cao Pi put down his own bow, moved, and from then on he thought of the boy as his heir.",
  "wei:battle": "In the winter of 208 Cao Cao (曹操) brought a vast northern army down the Yangtze (长江), sure that the south would fall. At Red Cliffs (赤壁) his men, unused to boats and stricken with disease, were scattered by the fire ships of Zhou Yu (周瑜) and Huang Gai (黄盖). Cao Cao retreated through the marshes on the Huarong (华容) road, where the mud was so deep that weak soldiers were trampled laying grass for the horses. The dream of reuniting China in one stroke ended there.",
  "wei:gather": "After years of civil war the north was empty and starving; soldiers ate mulberries and even each other. In 196 Cao Cao (曹操) accepted Zao Zhi's (枣祗) plan for military farms (屯田): refugees and soldiers were settled on abandoned land near Xuchang (许昌) and given oxen and seed. The first year brought in a million hu of grain. Gathering that harvest was the real foundation of Wei (魏): armies that could eat could fight, and families who could eat could stay.",
  "wei:ritual": "In the tenth month of 220 the last Han emperor, Xian, handed the throne to Cao Pi (曹丕). At Fanyang a raised altar (受禅台) was built. Cao Pi climbed it before the officials and the troops, received the seal, and burned offerings to Heaven to announce that the mandate had passed to Wei (魏). He is said to have remarked afterward: now I know what happened between Shun (舜) and Yu. Stone stelae recording the ceremony still stand at the site.",
  "wei:rain": "In 230 the Wei (魏) regent Cao Zhen (曹真) marched toward Shu (蜀) through the Ziwu (子午) valley, aiming to end the war in the west. Then the rain came: over thirty days without pause. The plank roads along the cliffs collapsed, supplies could not move, and the officials at court begged the emperor to recall the army. The soldiers sat in sodden camps watching the sky. The campaign was abandoned, and Cao Zhen fell ill and died the next year.",
  "wei:flood": "In 219 the general Yu Jin (于禁) camped with seven armies outside Fancheng (樊城). Autumn rains swelled the Han River (汉水) until it broke its banks, and his men were trapped on mounds of earth as the water rose. Guan Yu's (关羽) boats came for them. Yu Jin surrendered after thirty years of loyal service. His comrade Pang De (庞德) refused to kneel and was executed. Cao Cao (曹操) said he had known Yu Jin for thirty years and never expected that.",
  "wei:wind": "In 225 Cao Pi (曹丕) led a huge fleet to the Yangtze (长江) at Guangling (广陵) to invade Wu (吴). Winter storms whipped the river into great waves, ice formed, and his warships could not cross. Standing on the bank looking south, he sighed: Heaven itself made this river to divide north and south (固天所以限南北也). He turned back. The storm, as much as Wu's armies, kept China divided for another half century.",
  "wei:fire": "In 200 at Guandu (官渡), Cao Cao's (曹操) army was outnumbered by Yuan Shao's (袁绍) and running out of food. Then a defector, Xu You (许攸), told him where Yuan Shao's grain was stored: at Wuchao (乌巢). Cao Cao himself led five thousand men by night, carrying Yuan (袁) banners and bundles of firewood, and burned the depot. Yuan Shao's army collapsed. The fire that night decided who would rule northern China.",
  "wei:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm over Ye (邺), Cao Cao's (曹操) capital. On the Bronze Sparrow Terrace (铜雀台) the poets of the Jian'an (建安) circle paused their verses as lightning lit the walls and thunder rolled across the plain. Officials whispered about omens: was Heaven pleased with the house of Cao, or warning it? In the barracks the soldiers simply covered their heads and waited for the rain to pass, as they always had.",
  "wei:birth": "Cao Chong (曹冲), born around 196, was the child Cao Cao (曹操) loved most. At about five, when Sun Quan (孙权) sent an elephant, the boy weighed it by marking how low it set a boat in the water, then loading the boat with stones to the same mark (曹冲称象). Cao Cao hoped to make him heir. In 208 Cao Chong fell ill and died at twelve. Cao Cao told his other sons: this is my misfortune, but your good fortune.",
  "wei:death": "Before his death in 220 Cao Cao (曹操) left a will: bury me in the clothes of the season, put no gold, jade or treasure in my tomb, and let the troops not leave their posts to mourn. The world was still at war and he did not want resources wasted. In 2009 archaeologists in Anyang (安阳) identified his tomb, Gaoling, which indeed held modest goods: stone tablets naming his weapons, and the bones of a man of about sixty.",

  // ── Wu ──
  "wu:hunt": "Sun Quan (孙权), ruler of Wu (吴), loved to ride out and shoot tigers. Once a tiger leaped up and clawed at his saddle. His old minister Zhang Zhao (张昭) rebuked him sharply: a ruler should command heroes, not race wild beasts. Sun Quan apologized, but could not give it up; he had a special carriage built with a shooting window so he could hunt tigers from inside it. His brother Sun Ce (孙策) had been killed while hunting alone, and the court never forgot.",
  "wu:battle": "In the winter of 208 Cao Cao's (曹操) great northern fleet came down the Yangtze (长江). Many in Wu (吴) urged surrender. Sun Quan (孙权) drew his sword, cut the corner off his desk, and said anyone who mentioned surrender again would end like the desk. Zhou Yu (周瑜), just thirty-three, led the allied fleet with Liu Bei (刘备) at Red Cliffs (赤壁). Huang Gai's (黄盖) burning ships crashed into the chained enemy fleet. For Wu it was the day the south proved it could stand alone.",
  "wu:gather": "Imagined: in the wet fields of Jiangdong (江东), families of the Wu (吴) kingdom transplanted rice and waded the paddies at harvest, while the women raised silkworms through the warm months. The northerners had fled here from war in their tens of thousands, and the old southerners and the newcomers learned to farm side by side. Every boat of rice sent up to Jianye (建业) fed the soldiers who guarded the long river line.",
  "wu:ritual": "In 229 Sun Quan (孙权) finally declared himself emperor at Wuchang (武昌). Officials reported auspicious signs: a yellow dragon and phoenixes had been seen, and he named his era Huanglong (黄龙), Yellow Dragon. On the altar he offered to Heaven and announced that the mandate had come to the south as well. For a people long dismissed by northerners as marsh-dwellers, it was a solemn claim that Heaven saw them too.",
  "wu:rain": "In 222 Liu Bei's (刘备) avenging army camped deep in Wu (吴) territory. The young commander Lu Xun (陆逊) refused to fight. For months his generals, many older and nobler than he, raged at him for cowardice. Lu Xun simply waited, through spring rains into summer heat, until the Shu (蜀) soldiers grew tired and careless in their forest camps. He was waiting for the world to change, and when it did, he struck.",
  "wu:flood": "Imagined: one summer the Yangtze (长江) rose for weeks, brown and fast, flooding the low fields around Jianye (建业). Wu (吴) farmers dragged their boats to the doorsteps and carried grandparents and rice jars up to the dykes. Soldiers were sent from the garrisons to fill baskets with earth. When the water fell, they planted again in the fresh silt, saying, as Jiangdong (江东) people always had, that the river takes, and the river gives.",
  "wu:wind": "Imagined: a summer storm caught a Wu (吴) fleet on the Yangtze (长江) near Wuchang (武昌). The great tower ships (楼船) heeled as the gale tore at their sails; oarsmen drove for the shore while others prayed to the river spirits. Wu ruled by the water, and its sailors knew how quickly the river could turn on them. When the wind dropped, they counted the missing boats and made offerings at the riverside shrine.",
  "wu:fire": "In the summer of 222 Liu Bei's (刘备) army stretched its wooden camps along hundreds of li of forested valleys at Yiling (夷陵). Lu Xun (陆逊), who had waited for months, ordered each soldier to carry a bundle of reeds and set fire to the camps. The fire leapt from fort to fort. Over forty Shu (蜀) camps fell and Liu Bei fled. Lu Xun, the young scholar mocked by older generals, became the hero of Wu (吴).",
  "wu:thunder": "Imagined: in the hot summers of Jiangdong (江东), storms build fast over the Yangtze (长江) and break in the afternoon. Wu (吴) villagers hurried the buffalo home, closed the shutters and lit incense before the thunder god's image. Sailors tied up their boats. Children were told that the thunder searched for wrongdoers and that the good had nothing to fear, though the grown-ups looked up at the sky a little nervously all the same.",
  "wu:birth": "The Records of the Three Kingdoms says that when Lady Wu (吴夫人), wife of the warrior Sun Jian (孙坚), was pregnant with her first son Sun Ce (孙策), she dreamed the moon entered her body; when pregnant with Sun Quan (孙权), she dreamed of the sun. Sun Jian said the sun and moon are the essence of yin and yang, and his descendants would rise high. Both sons grew to rule the south, and Sun Quan founded the state of Wu.",
  "wu:death": "In 200 Sun Ce (孙策), conqueror of the south, was ambushed while hunting and died of his wounds at twenty-five. On his deathbed he gave the seal to his eighteen-year-old brother Sun Quan (孙权): in leading the armies of Jiangdong (江东) you are not my equal, but in employing worthy men I am not yours. Sun Quan wept without stopping. Zhang Zhao (张昭) told him that grief must wait; he dressed him, put him on a horse and had him review the troops.",

  // ── Zhu Xi's Legacy ──
  "zhu-xis-legacy:hunt": "Imagined: in the hills of Fujian (福建) under the Southern Song (南宋), a scholar's household went out in winter with nets and dogs after hares and pheasants. The young men had read in the Book of Rites (礼记) that one must not hunt in spring, when animals bear young, nor surround a herd on all sides. Their teacher, a follower of Zhu Xi (朱熹), reminded them that even in a hunt one investigates things (格物) and keeps a humane heart.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:battle": "In 1279 at Yamen (崖山), off the coast of Guangdong (广东), the last Song fleet of about a thousand ships was chained together against the Mongol-led Yuan (元) navy. After weeks of siege and thirst, the line broke. The minister Lu Xiufu (陆秀夫), a Confucian scholar raised on Zhu Xi's (朱熹) teaching of loyalty, took the eight-year-old emperor on his back and leapt into the sea. Many officials and families followed. Song scholars long remembered it as the death of a civilization.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:gather": "In 1171, after famine in his district of Wufu (五夫) in Fujian (福建), Zhu Xi (朱熹) set up a community granary (社仓). In good years the villagers gathered grain into it; in hungry spring months they borrowed it at low interest and repaid after harvest. It was Confucian ethics made practical: neighbours caring for neighbours without waiting for the state. Zhu Xi asked the court to copy it everywhere, and community granaries spread across China.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:ritual": "In 1195, as the minister Han Tuozhou (韩侂胄) persecuted the so-called false learning (伪学), Zhu Xi (朱熹) drafted a long memorial attacking him. His students begged him not to send it. Finally Zhu Xi took out the yarrow stalks and divined with the Book of Changes (易经). The answer was the hexagram Dun (遯): retreat. He burned the draft in silence and took the name Dunweng (遯翁), the Old Man Who Retreats. He treated divination not as magic but as a serious consultation with the patterns of Heaven.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:rain": "In 1179 Zhu Xi (朱熹) became prefect of Nankang (南康), at the foot of Mount Lu (庐山), and the next year drought struck. As a magistrate he led prayers for rain, as custom required, but he also cut taxes, opened granaries, organized relief and sent reports pleading for aid. He wrote that a ruler must feel the people's hunger as his own. He and his officials walked the dry fields each day, waiting for clouds and doing what human hands could do meanwhile.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:flood": "Imagined: one summer under the Southern Song (南宋), a mountain torrent swept down a valley near Wuyi (武夷), where Zhu Xi (朱熹) had once taught. The villagers fled to the hillside with their ancestral tablets in their arms before anything else. Afterward the local scholars organized repairs to the dykes and distributed grain, reading aloud from their teacher's writings that true learning must show itself in helping one's community.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:wind": "Imagined: a typhoon blew in from the sea over the Fujian (福建) coast, tearing roofs from the houses at Jianyang (建阳) where Song printers carved the woodblocks of Zhu Xi's (朱熹) commentaries. The printers hurried to cover the blocks with oiled cloth. Afterward the scholars walked the town, and one remarked that wind too is only qi in motion, following its principle (理), and that a calm heart can weather any storm.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:fire": "Lin'an (临安), the Southern Song (南宋) capital at Hangzhou (杭州), was a crowded city of wooden houses, and great fires struck it again and again. Watchtowers stood across the city, and fire brigades of soldiers rushed out with buckets, axes and ladders at the first smoke. Officials who followed Zhu Xi's (朱熹) teaching asked whether the fires were Heaven's warning about misgovernment, and urged the emperor to examine his conduct and relieve the homeless.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:thunder": "Zhu Xi's (朱熹) students once asked him what thunder is. He answered, as recorded in the Classified Conversations (朱子语类), that it is the clash of yin and yang qi: when yang is trapped and bursts out, there is thunder; it is like firing a gun. He did not deny spirits entirely, but he taught that the world follows principle (理) and can be understood. For his followers, a thunderstorm was awe-inspiring, but also something to study calmly.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:birth": "Zhu Xi (朱熹) was born in 1130 at Youxi (尤溪) in Fujian (福建), while northern China was overrun by the Jurchen Jin (金) and his family had fled south. Later followers said he had seven moles on his right cheek arranged like the Big Dipper (北斗). His father, Zhu Song (朱松), was a minor official who opposed peace with the Jin. No one then could know this child would shape Chinese, Korean and Japanese thought for seven hundred years.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:death": "Zhu Xi (朱熹) died in 1200 while his learning was still banned as false (庆元党禁). The court warned officials against gathering at his funeral, yet nearly a thousand people came to Jianyang (建阳) to bury him according to the rites he had written down in his Family Rituals (朱子家礼). The poet Xin Qiji (辛弃疾) wrote in his eulogy that what never decays is a name handed down for ten thousand generations. Within decades his commentaries became the basis of the civil examinations.",

  // ── Japanese ──
  "japanese:hunt": "In 1193 Minamoto no Yoritomo (源頼朝), the new shogun, held a grand hunt at the foot of Mount Fuji (富士の巻狩り). Warriors drove deer and boar toward the archers for weeks, and his son Yoriie's (頼家) first kill was celebrated as a sign from the gods. On the last night, the Soga brothers (曾我兄弟), Sukenari (祐成) and Tokimune (時宗), slipped into the camp in the rain and killed Kudo Suketsune (工藤祐経), who had murdered their father eighteen years earlier. The tale of their revenge is still performed on the kabuki stage.",
  "japanese-early:battle": "In 1185 the Taira and Minamoto (源) fleets met at Dan-no-ura (壇ノ浦) in the strait of Shimonoseki (下関). When the tide turned against them, the Taira saw that all was lost. The grandmother of the six-year-old emperor Antoku (安徳天皇) took him in her arms; he asked where they were going, and she told him there was a capital beneath the waves. They sank together. The Tale of the Heike (平家) begins with a temple bell ringing of the impermanence of all things.",
  "japanese:gather": "Each June in the rice villages of Japan came taue (田植え), the planting. Women called saotome, in matching kimono and straw hats, bent in a line through the flooded paddies, setting seedlings by hand while drummers and flute players on the banks played rice-planting songs (田植え歌) to keep the rhythm and please the god of the fields. At autumn harvest the first grain went to the shrine. Gathering rice was never only work: it was a festival with the gods.",
  "japanese:ritual": "At Ise Jingu (伊勢神宮), Japan's holiest shrine, the sanctuary of the sun goddess Amaterasu (天照) is rebuilt from fresh cypress every twenty years in the Shikinen Sengu (式年遷宮), a custom traced to 690. On the night of the transfer, priests in white carry the sacred mirror, hidden under silk screens, from the old shrine to the new in darkness and silence. It is a prayer that the land will be renewed, and a promise between generations.",
  "japanese-early:rain": "In 824, as drought parched the capital, the court asked two great monks to pray for rain at the Shinsen-en (神泉苑) garden in Kyoto (京都). Legend says the monk Shubin (守敏) had trapped all the dragon gods in a jar, but Kukai (空海) found one who had escaped, the dragon queen Zennyo, and invited her from the Himalayas. Rain fell across Japan for three days. Farmers long held rain-calling rites (雨乞い) at village ponds, drums beating, waiting for the clouds.",
  "japanese:flood": "The Kamanashi River (釜無川) in Kai (甲斐) flooded almost every year, ruining the rice plains. In the 1540s the warlord Takeda Shingen (武田信玄) began building a system of staggered levees, stone spurs and redirected channels that turned the floods aside. Villagers who settled behind it were excused from taxes in return for guarding it. Parts of the Shingen Levee (信玄堤) still protect Kofu (甲府) today, and the people of Yamanashi (山梨) still speak of him with gratitude.",
  "japanese:wind": "In the summer of 1281, more than four thousand ships of the Mongol-led Yuan (元) fleet gathered off Hakata Bay (博多湾). For weeks samurai had fought them from the stone wall built along the shore. Then, in mid-August, a great typhoon struck, sinking most of the fleet. Japanese temples and shrines had been praying for months, and the people called it the divine wind (神風, kamikaze): proof that the gods protected the land. Relief swept the country like the storm itself.",
  "japanese:fire": "On the eighteenth day of the first month of 1657, a fire started in Hongo in Edo and was driven by strong winds through a city dry from a winter without rain. Over three days the Meireki Fire (明暦の大火) burned most of the capital, including the main keep of Edo Castle (江戸城), and killed perhaps a hundred thousand people. Legend blamed a cursed long-sleeved kimono (振袖火事) being burned at a temple. Edo rebuilt with firebreaks, wider streets and fire brigades.",
  "japanese-early:thunder": "Sugawara no Michizane (菅原道真), a great scholar exiled unjustly in 901, died in Dazaifu (大宰府) in 903. Soon his enemies began to die, and in 930 lightning struck the Seiryoden (清涼殿) of the imperial palace, killing several courtiers. The court believed his angry spirit had become the thunder god. He was pardoned, promoted after death, and enshrined as Tenjin (天神), now the god of learning. People say kuwabara, kuwabara during storms, because lightning was said never to strike his old estate.",
  "japanese:birth": "In the fifth month of pregnancy, on a day of the dog (戌の日), a Japanese mother-to-be visited a shrine such as Suitengu (水天宮) in Tokyo (東京) and was wrapped in a long white sash (岩田帯), because dogs give birth easily and many pups survive. Childbirth was dangerous, and families prayed hard. After a month the baby was carried to the local shrine for its first visit (お宮参り), and the family asked the gods to watch over whatever life this child would lead.",
  "japanese:death": "Most Japanese funerals follow Buddhist custom. The family keeps an all-night vigil (通夜), a priest gives the dead a new Buddhist name (戒名), and the body is cremated. Then comes kotsuage (骨上げ): two relatives together lift the bones with long chopsticks into the urn, starting from the feet so the dead will not be upside down. Each summer at Obon (お盆) the ancestors are welcomed home with lanterns and dances, then sent back with fires.",

  // ── Yamato ──
  "yamato:hunt": "The Kojiki (古事記) tells that Emperor Yuryaku (雄略天皇), in the fifth century, went hunting on Mount Katsuragi (葛城山) with his court dressed in blue robes and red cords. Across the valley came another procession exactly like his own. Angry, the emperor demanded its leader's name, and the stranger answered that he was Hitokotonushi (一言主), the god who settles good and bad with a single word. Awed, Yuryaku bowed, offered his sword, bow and the clothes of his men, and the god escorted him home.",
  "yamato:battle": "In 663 Yamato (大和) sent a great fleet to help restore the fallen kingdom of Baekje (百済) in Korea. At the mouth of the Baekgang river, called Hakusukinoe (白村江) in Japan, they met the Tang (唐) and Silla (新羅) navies. The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) says the Yamato ships charged in disorder and were caught between the enemy lines; in a short time many drowned, and the river ran red. Fearing invasion, the court built stone fortresses and beacon fires across Kyushu (九州).",
  "yamato:gather": "The first poem of the Man'yoshu (万葉集) is said to be by Emperor Yuryaku (雄略天皇). He meets a young woman gathering spring greens on a hill: With a basket, a pretty basket, with a trowel, a pretty trowel, you maiden picking herbs on this hillside, tell me your house, tell me your name. Gathering young herbs in early spring was a seasonal custom in Yamato (大和), and the poem catches its brightness: fresh shoots, open skies, a new year beginning.",
  "yamato:ritual": "The Chinese Records of Wei describe the land of Wa (倭) in the third century, ruled by the queen Himiko (卑弥呼), who served the way of the spirits and bewitched the people. Before any journey or undertaking, the Wa heated bones over fire and read the cracks to tell good fortune from bad. In Japan this became futomani (太占): the shoulder blade of a deer burned over cherry bark, its cracks asking the gods what would come.",
  "yamato:rain": "In 642 a severe drought struck Yamato (大和). The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) says the powerful minister Soga no Emishi (蘇我蝦夷) had Buddhist sutras read and incense burned, and only a light rain fell. Then Empress Kogyoku (皇極天皇) went to the riverhead at Minabuchi (南淵), knelt, bowed to the four directions and prayed to Heaven. Thunder rolled and heavy rain fell for five days, reaching the whole land. The farmers praised her as an empress of supreme virtue.",
  "yamato:flood": "The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) says Emperor Nintoku (仁徳天皇) built the Mamuta embankment (茨田堤) against the floods of the Yodo River (淀川), but two gaps kept breaking. He dreamed that a god demanded two men be sacrificed. One man, Kowakubi, was thrown in. The other, Koromono-ko, threw two gourds into the water and said: if you are truly a god, sink these; if they float, you are false. The gourds floated, he lived, and the dyke held.",
  "yamato:wind": "Yamato's (大和) envoys to Tang China (遣唐使) crossed the sea in fragile ships, and many died in storms. The Chinese monk Jianzhen (鑑真, Ganjin) was invited to Japan in 742; five voyages failed to typhoons and shipwreck, and he went blind on the way. On his sixth attempt, in 753, he finally reached Japan and founded Toshodai-ji (唐招提寺) in Nara (奈良). The envoy Abe no Nakamaro (阿倍仲麻呂), on the same fleet, was blown off course and never returned home.",
  "yamato:fire": "The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) records that in the fourth month of 670, in the middle of the night, the temple of Horyu-ji (法隆寺), founded by Prince Shotoku (聖徳太子), caught fire and not a single building was left. Heavy rain and thunder followed. The temple was rebuilt in the following decades, and today its wooden halls are among the oldest surviving in the world, still carrying the devotion of the people who would not let it vanish.",
  "yamato:thunder": "The Nihon Ryoiki (日本霊異記) tells that Emperor Yuryaku (雄略天皇) once asked his attendant Sugaru (栖軽) to fetch the thunder god. Sugaru rode out with a red banner calling for the god to come in the emperor's name. Lightning fell on a hill, and Sugaru brought the thunder god back in a palanquin. The emperor, frightened by its brilliance, had it returned. The place was named Ikazuchi-no-oka (雷丘), Thunder Hill, still in Asuka (飛鳥) today.",
  "yamato:birth": "Legend in the Kojiki (古事記) and Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) says Empress Jingu (神功皇后) was pregnant when she led an expedition across the sea to Korea. To delay the birth until her return, she tied stones to her waist. She came home and gave birth in Tsukushi (筑紫) to the future Emperor Ojin (応神天皇), later worshipped as the war god Hachiman (八幡). Her stones, the chinkaiseki (鎮懐石), were revered as charms for safe childbirth by Yamato (大和) mothers for centuries.",
  "yamato:death": "Yamato (大和) rulers were buried in great keyhole-shaped mounds (前方後円墳) like the tomb attributed to Emperor Nintoku (仁徳天皇), among the largest graves on earth. Their slopes were lined with clay figures called haniwa (埴輪). The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) says that when Empress Hibasu-hime (日葉酢媛) died, the craftsman Nomi no Sukune (野見宿禰) proposed clay figures instead of burying servants alive, and the emperor agreed. Rows of haniwa warriors, horses and houses watched over the dead.",

  // ── Sengoku Daimyo ──
  "sengoku-daimyo:hunt": "Oda Nobunaga (織田信長) loved falconry (鷹狩り). The Shincho Koki (信長公記) chronicle records him riding out again and again with his hawks, and he once held a hunt with hundreds of falconers. Hunting grounds also let a lord inspect his land, test his men and show the peasants his power. Tokugawa Ieyasu (徳川家康), who also adored falconry, said it kept the body strong and let him see how the farmers lived. For the warlords, the hunt was rest and strategy in one.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:battle": "In 1561, at the fourth battle of Kawanakajima (川中島), the armies of Takeda Shingen (武田信玄) and Uesugi Kenshin (上杉謙信) met in morning fog. Legend says Kenshin rode alone into Shingen's headquarters and struck at him three times with his sword; Shingen, still seated, parried with his iron war fan. Both lost thousands that day. The two rivals fought five times over a decade and never decided anything, but the story of their duel was told for centuries.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:gather": "In 1588 Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉) ordered the sword hunt (刀狩): every farmer in Japan must hand over swords, bows, spears and guns. The edict said the metal would be melted down for nails in the great Buddha he was building in Kyoto (京都), so the farmers would be saved in this life and the next. In truth it ended a century in which peasants fought as warriors. Gathering weapons, Hideyoshi gathered the whole country under one hand.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:ritual": "Uesugi Kenshin (上杉謙信) believed himself an avatar of Bishamonten (毘沙門天), the Buddhist god of war, and flew a banner with the character 毘. Before battles he shut himself in the Bishamon hall at Kasugayama (春日山) castle for days of prayer and fasting. Many daimyo also kept gunbaisha, military advisers who chose lucky days and directions by divination. Asking the gods before a battle was as natural as checking the weapons.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:rain": "In 1560 Imagawa Yoshimoto (今川義元) marched on Kyoto (京都) with an army of perhaps twenty-five thousand. Oda Nobunaga (織田信長), with only a few thousand, prayed at Atsuta Shrine (熱田神宮) and then waited in the hills above Okehazama (桶狭間). Around midday a violent rain and hail storm broke over the valley. When it cleared, Nobunaga charged into Yoshimoto's resting camp, and Yoshimoto was killed. The world had changed in one storm.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:flood": "In 1582 Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉) besieged Takamatsu (高松) castle in Bitchu (備中), set in marshland. Instead of storming it, his men built a long earthen dam in about twelve days and diverted the rivers; the rainy season filled the plain, and the castle became an island (水攻め). To save his garrison, the commander Shimizu Muneharu (清水宗治) rowed out in a small boat, performed a final dance, and took his own life before both armies.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:wind": "Imagined: in the eighth month a typhoon swept across the rice plains of a Sengoku (戦国) domain just before harvest. The lord's retainers rode through the villages afterward counting flattened fields, and the castle granary was opened to the hungriest. The headmen begged for lower taxes. With enemies on every border, the daimyo knew that a ruined harvest could be as dangerous as a lost battle, and he sent rice south to buy peace.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:fire": "Before dawn on the second day of the sixth month of 1582, Akechi Mitsuhide's (明智光秀) troops surrounded Honno-ji (本能寺) temple in Kyoto (京都), where Oda Nobunaga (織田信長) was staying with a small guard. When told who the attackers were, Nobunaga is recorded to have said: it cannot be helped (是非に及ばず). He fought with bow and spear, then withdrew inside as the temple burned. His body was never found. The man who had almost united Japan vanished in the flames.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:thunder": "Tachibana Dosetsu (立花道雪), a retainer of the Otomo (大友) clan in Kyushu (九州), was resting under a tree during a storm when lightning struck. He drew his sword Chidori (千鳥) and, legend says, cut the thunder god inside the bolt. He was left lame but alive, and his sword was renamed Raikiri (雷切), Lightning Cutter. In an age of endless war, the Sengoku (戦国) warriors loved a story in which even the sky could be fought.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:birth": "In 1593 Lady Yodo (淀殿) gave birth to a son, Hiroi (拾), later named Hideyori (豊臣秀頼). His father, Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉), was fifty-seven and had lost his first son. Overjoyed, he wrote affectionate letters to the baby and later forced his own nephew and heir to commit suicide. Before his death he made the great lords swear to protect the boy. In 1615 Osaka (大坂) castle fell and Hideyori died at twenty-two, with all the hope placed on him.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:death": "Takeda Shingen (武田信玄) died in 1573 during a campaign, perhaps of illness. His will, as the Koyo Gunkan (甲陽軍鑑) records, ordered that his death be kept secret for three years, so his enemies would not take advantage. A double sat in his place, and letters went out under his seal. His body is said to have been sunk in Lake Suwa (諏訪湖) in armor, or buried quietly at Erin-ji (恵林寺). His retainers mourned in silence, guarding the secret.",

  // ── Koreans ──
  "koreans:hunt": "In 1404 King Taejong of Joseon, out hunting with hawks and hounds, fell from his horse while chasing a deer. As he got up, his first words to his attendants were: do not let the historian know. But the court historian had already heard, and the Annals of the Joseon Dynasty (조선왕조실록) record both the fall and the king's order not to record it. For Koreans the story became a proud symbol of honest history that even a king could not bend.",
  "koreans:battle": "In 1597 Admiral Yi Sun-sin (이순신), just restored to command after torture and demotion, had only thirteen ships left. He wrote to the king: your servant still has twelve ships. At the Myeongnyang strait (명량), where the tide roars like a river, he faced some 133 Japanese warships. He held his flagship in the current while the others hesitated, then the tide turned and the enemy line broke. Koreans still call it the miracle of Myeongnyang.",
  "koreans:gather": "In the deep mountains of Korea, ginseng hunters called simmani (심마니) searched for wild ginseng (산삼). They kept strict taboos: purifying themselves beforehand, sleeping apart, speaking a secret language, and praying to the mountain god (산신). When one finally spotted the red berries, he shouted Simbatda! (심봤다, I have seen the root), and the others knelt. They dug it out slowly with deer-horn picks so no hair root would break.",
  "koreans:ritual": "At Jongmyo (종묘) shrine in Seoul (서울), the spirit tablets of the Joseon (조선) kings and queens are honored in the Jongmyo Jerye (종묘제례), a royal ancestral rite performed for six centuries. Descendants in ceremonial robes offer food and wine in strict order as court musicians play Jongmyo Jeryeak (종묘제례악), slow and grave, with stone chimes and bronze bells. It is not mournful: it is a solemn conversation with ancestors, asking them to watch over the land.",
  "koreans:rain": "In 1441, during the reign of King Sejong (세종), Korea created a standard rain gauge (측우기): a bronze cylinder placed in every province to measure rainfall precisely, about two centuries before Europe. Kings still led rain-prayer ceremonies (기우제) during droughts, and Sejong himself fasted for his people. But the gauges let officials plan taxes and relief by actual measurement. It was a mix of prayer and science, both aimed at the farmers' fields.",
  "koreans:wind": "In 1653 a Dutch East India ship, the Sperwer, was wrecked by a storm off Jeju Island (제주도). Thirty-six sailors reached shore. The Korean authorities sent them to Seoul (서울), where they were kept for thirteen years; Joseon (조선) did not let outsiders leave. In 1666 Hendrick Hamel and seven others escaped by boat to Japan, and his journal gave Europe its first detailed account of Korea. A storm opened a door both peoples had kept shut.",
  "koreans:thunder": "Imagined: on a summer afternoon in a Joseon (조선) village, a sudden storm broke over the rice paddies. A grandmother called the children inside and told them that the thunder was the thunder god (뇌신) searching for those who had wronged their parents. The children sat very still while the rain drummed on the thatched roof, and the grandmother laughed and gave them roasted chestnuts when the thunder moved on.",
  "koreans:birth": "When a baby was born in a Korean house, a straw rope called geumjul (금줄) was hung across the gate: with red chili peppers and charcoal for a boy, pine sprigs and charcoal for a girl. It told neighbors not to enter for three weeks, keeping illness away. The family prayed to Samsin halmeoni (삼신할머니), the birth grandmother goddess. At a hundred days came baegil (백일), and at the first birthday the doljabi (돌잡이), when the child chooses its future from a table of objects.",
  "koreans:death": "In a Joseon Confucian funeral the mourners wore coarse hemp and wailed aigo, aigo (곡) at set times. The coffin was carried on a colorful bier called sangyeo (상여), decorated with paper flowers, while a lead singer rang a bell and sang the bier song (상엿소리) and the bearers answered in chorus: now I go, leaving this world. The dead were buried on a hillside chosen by geomancy, and the family performed rites (제사) on every anniversary.",

  // ── Choson (Gojoseon) ──
  "choson:hunt": "Imagined: in the forests of Liaodong (요동) and the northern peninsula, the people of Gojoseon (고조선) hunted deer and boar with bows of wood and horn, some tipped with bronze. A young man's first kill was carried home and shared out among the families of the village. An elder offered its blood to the mountain spirit and spoke a word for the animal, so the forest would keep giving. Then they feasted under the stars.",
  "choson:battle": "In 109 BC Emperor Wu of Han sent armies by land and sea against Wiman Joseon (위만조선). King Ugeo (우거왕) held his walled capital of Wanggeom (왕검성) for nearly a year; the Han generals quarrelled and failed. But inside the city, ministers who wanted peace finally killed the king in 108 BC. Even then the minister Seonggi (성기) kept resisting until he too was killed. The city fell, and Han set up its commanderies. The kingdom ended not in the field but from within.",
  "choson:gather": "Imagined: hundreds of villagers gathered at a riverside in the Korean Bronze Age to raise a dolmen (고인돌) for a respected chief. They had split a massive capstone from a cliff using wooden wedges soaked in water, dragged it on log rollers with ropes, singing in rhythm to pull together, and slid it onto the upright stones over a ramp of earth. Korea holds tens of thousands of dolmens, among the most in the world.",
  "choson:ritual": "In the founding myth, Dangun Wanggeom (단군왕검) was both priest and king: dangun may mean the one who serves heaven, wanggeom the ruler. On Mani mountain on Ganghwa Island (강화도) stands the stone altar Chamseongdan (참성단), where tradition says Dangun offered sacrifices to Heaven. Koreans still light a sacred flame there for national festivals and celebrate Gaecheonjeol (개천절), the day heaven opened, each October 3.",
  "choson:rain": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells that Hwanung (환웅), son of the heavenly ruler, descended to Mount Taebaek (태백산) bringing three thousand followers and three lords: the Earl of Wind (풍백), the Master of Rain (우사) and the Master of Clouds (운사). Through them he governed grain, life, sickness and justice. For a farming people, the founding of the nation began with the gods of weather themselves coming down to share the waiting for rain.",
  "choson:flood": "Imagined: a summer of heavy rain swelled the Taedong River (대동강) until it broke into the fields around the Gojoseon (고조선) settlements. The villagers carried their pottery, seed grain and bronze daggers up to the hill fort and sheltered there for days. When the water receded, the elders gathered at the dolmen of the old chief to offer thanks that no one had drowned, and the families went down together to rebuild their pit houses.",
  "choson:wind": "Imagined: a great autumn storm swept down from the steppe over the plains of Liaodong (요동). In the Gojoseon (고조선) villages the wind tore thatch from the pit houses and flattened the millet. The people believed it was the Earl of Wind (풍백), who had descended with Hwanung (환웅), reminding them of heaven's power. The shaman hung offerings on the sacred tree at the village edge, and everyone waited for the sky to be calm again.",
  "choson:fire": "Imagined: in the last days of Wanggeom (왕검성) in 108 BC, fires were lit along the walls as Han armies pressed the siege. Inside, families buried bronze mirrors and daggers beneath their floors, hoping to return for them. Two thousand years later archaeologists would find such hoards. The people of Joseon (조선) watched the smoke rise over their capital, unsure whether surrender would bring peace or slavery.",
  "choson:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm crashed over the Gojoseon (고조선) heartland, and lightning split an old oak on the ridge where the ancestors were buried. The villagers gathered in fear: was Heaven, the realm of Hwanin (환인), angry with them? The shaman danced with bells and a bronze mirror, flashing it toward the sky, and offered millet and wine. By morning the storm had passed, and the split oak was bound with straw rope as a sacred tree.",
  "choson:birth": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells that a bear and a tiger begged Hwanung (환웅) to become human. He gave them mugwort and twenty cloves of garlic and told them to stay out of the sunlight for a hundred days. The tiger gave up; the bear endured and became a woman, Ungnyeo (웅녀). She prayed under the sandalwood tree for a child, and Hwanung married her. Their son was Dangun (단군), who founded Joseon (조선), traditionally in 2333 BC. A whole people was born of her patience.",
  "choson:death": "The people of Gojoseon (고조선) and the Korean Bronze Age buried their dead under dolmens (고인돌) and in stone-cist graves, often with mandolin-shaped bronze daggers (비파형동검) and fine bronze mirrors. The dolmen fields of Gochang (고창), Hwasun (화순) and Ganghwa (강화) are now a UNESCO World Heritage site. Dangun (단군) himself, the legend says, did not die but withdrew to the mountain at the age of 1908 and became its mountain god (산신).",

  // ── Mongols ──
  "mongols:hunt": "In early winter the Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) army held the great battue hunt, the nerge. Juvaini describes it: the riders spread in a ring many days' march wide, then closed it slowly over weeks, commanded by flags and drums, until all the game was trapped in a circle of men. Any soldier who let an animal escape was punished. The khan shot first, then the princes, then the troops; the last animals were set free. It was a feast, a census of the steppe and a rehearsal for war.",
  "mongols:battle": "In April 1241 the Mongol army under Batu (ᠪᠠᠲᠤ / Бат) and Subutai (ᠰᠦᠪᠡᠭᠡᠳᠡᠢ / Сүбээдэй) met King Bela IV of Hungary at the Sajo river near Mohi. At dawn Batu's men forced the bridge with stone-throwing catapults while Subutai crossed secretly downstream. The Hungarians were penned in their wagon camp; the Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) left a gap open in their ring, and as the enemy fled through it, they rode them down along the road. For the steppe riders it was proof that nothing in the west could stop them.",
  "mongols:gather": "In the spring of 1206 the Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) clans gathered at the source of the Onon (ᠣᠨᠠᠨ / Онон) river for a great kurultai (assembly). The white standard with nine tails was raised, and Temujin (ᠲᠡᠮᠦᠵᠢᠨ / Тэмүжин) was proclaimed Chinggis Khan (ᠴᠢᠩᠭᠢᠰ ᠬᠠᠭᠠᠨ / Чингис хаан), ruler of all the people of the felt walls, as the Secret History (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ ᠤᠨ ᠨᠢᠭᠤᠴᠠ ᠲᠣᠪᠴᠢᠶᠠᠨ / Монголын нууц товчоо) calls them. Feuding tribes became one nation. Every summer afterward the herders gathered again for horse races, wrestling and archery, the games that live on as Naadam (ᠨᠠᠭᠠᠳᠣᠮ / Наадам).",
  "mongols:ritual": "The Franciscan William of Rubruck, at the court of Mongke Khan (ᠮᠥᠩᠬᠡ / Мөнх хаан) in 1254, saw that the khan did nothing without first consulting a sheep's shoulder blade. Diviners laid the bones in fire until they cracked; if the cracks ran straight lengthwise, the khan would act; if they ran crosswise, he would not. The Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) worshipped Eternal Blue Heaven (Mongke Tengri) and spoke to it through shamans (boo), who beat drums and called the spirits.",
  "mongols:rain": "Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) believed some shamans could command the weather with a magic bezoar called the jada stone. Chronicles say that in 1232 at Sanfeng mountain (三峰山), as Tolui's (ᠲᠣᠯᠤᠢ / Тулуй) tired army faced a far larger Jin force, it began to snow and did not stop for three days; later accounts credited a weather-maker with his stone. The frozen Jin troops collapsed. For ordinary herders, waiting for rain meant watching the grass and praying that spring would come before the animals starved.",
  "mongols:wind": "In 1274 and again in 1281 Kublai Khan (ᠬᠤᠪᠢᠯᠠᠢ / Хубилай хаан) sent fleets of Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол), Chinese and Koreans to conquer Japan. Both times storms came. In 1281, after weeks of fighting at Hakata Bay, a typhoon struck; ship after ship, many hastily built from river boats, was smashed together and sunk, and tens of thousands drowned or were left on the shore. For the Mongols, who had conquered from Korea to Hungary, it was a bitter lesson: the sea and the wind did not obey the khan.",
  "mongols:fire": "In 1220 Chinggis Khan (ᠴᠢᠩᠭᠢᠰ ᠬᠠᠭᠠᠨ / Чингис хаан) took Bukhara. Ibn al-Athir and Juvaini tell that he rode into the great mosque, climbed the pulpit, and told the people: I am the punishment of God; if you had not committed great sins, God would not have sent me. The city of wooden houses then burned, and only the brick mosque and palaces were left. For the Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) it was terror used as strategy; for Bukhara it was the end of a world.",
  "mongols:thunder": "European envoys were struck by how much the Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) feared thunder. John of Plano Carpini (1246) and William of Rubruck (1254) wrote that in a storm the Mongols drove strangers out of their tents, wrapped themselves in black felt, and hid until it passed. Anyone killed by lightning, and his tent and goods, had to be purified by passing between two fires. Mongol law also forbade bathing in rivers or washing clothes in summer, lest it anger the thunder.",
  "mongols:birth": "The Secret History (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ ᠤᠨ ᠨᠢᠭᠤᠴᠠ ᠲᠣᠪᠴᠢᠶᠠᠨ / Монголын нууц товчоо) of the Mongols (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) tells that Temujin (ᠲᠡᠮᠦᠵᠢᠨ / Тэмүжин) was born by the Onon (ᠣᠨᠠᠨ / Онон) river, at Deluun Boldog (ᠳᠡᠯᠢᠭᠦᠨ ᠪᠣᠯᠳᠠᠭ / Дэлүүн болдог), holding in his right fist a blood clot the size of a knucklebone. It was taken as a sign of a conqueror. His father Yesugei (ᠶᠢᠰᠦᠭᠡᠢ / Есүхэй) named him after a Tatar (ᠲᠠᠲᠠᠷ / Татар) chief he had just captured. Some saw a great destiny; others, perhaps, a bloody one. No one could know yet that the boy would unite the steppe and build the largest land empire in history.",
  "mongols:death": "Chinggis Khan (ᠴᠢᠩᠭᠢᠰ ᠬᠠᠭᠠᠨ / Чингис хаан) died in 1227 during his campaign against Xi Xia. His body was carried back to Mongolia (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) and buried secretly near the sacred mountain Burkhan Khaldun (ᠪᠤᠷᠬᠠᠨ ᠬᠠᠯᠳᠤᠨ / Бурхан Халдун). Marco Polo heard that the escort killed everyone they met on the way, and later tradition says horses were driven over the grave until it vanished and a forest grew over it. The Mongols honored him through the Eight White Tents (Ordos), where his spirit is still served today.",

  // ── Jurchens ──
  "jurchens:hunt": "The Liao (辽) emperors prized the gyrfalcon haidongqing (海东青), the swift white falcon of the Jurchen (女真) lands. Every year Liao envoys came demanding them, and also demanding to be entertained by Jurchen women. Jurchen hunters had to fight neighbouring tribes along the falcon road to the coast to catch the birds. Resentment grew year by year. The falcons that hunted swans for the Liao court helped spark the uprising that destroyed the Liao.",
  "jurchens:battle": "In 1115 the Jurchen (女真) chieftain Wanyan Aguda (完颜阿骨打), newly proclaimed emperor of Jin (金), faced the Liao (辽) emperor Tianzuo's (天祚帝) huge army at Hubudagang (护步答冈). The Jin had perhaps twenty thousand horsemen; the Liao numbers were reported as hundreds of thousands. Aguda cut his face with a knife and told his men that they must fight to the death or be destroyed. The Jurchen cavalry charged the Liao centre and broke it. The Liao never recovered.",
  "jurchens:gather": "The Jurchen rivers yielded the pearls of the north (北珠), taken from mussels in the icy Songhua (松花江) and Amur (黑龙江) rivers, prized at the Liao (辽) and Song (宋) courts. Divers broke through the ice in late autumn to gather them, and the work killed many. Jurchens (女真) also gathered ginseng, sable furs and honey in the forests. Much of it went as tribute to their overlords, and the Jurchens bitterly remembered every load when the time came to rebel.",
  "jurchens:ritual": "A Song (宋) chronicle of the 1100s, the Sanchao Beimeng Huibian (三朝北盟会编), records that among the Jurchens (女真), shanman (珊蛮) is the Jurchen word for a female shaman, and that they could communicate with the spirits. It is one of the earliest written traces of the word shaman. Jurchen shamans beat drums and wore bells and mirrors, asking the spirits of heaven, mountains and ancestors about sickness, war and the future. The Manchu court kept these rites until 1912.",
  "jurchens:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer in the Jin (金) dynasty, Jurchen (女真) villagers in Manchuria (满洲) watched their millet wilt under a cloudless sky. The shaman set up a willow pole in the courtyard, the women brought wine and cakes, and the drum beat late into the night as she called on the spirits of heaven. The men went out each dawn to look at the mountains in the north, waiting for the clouds that would tell them the gods had heard.",
  "jurchens:flood": "In 1194, under the Jin (金) dynasty ruled by the Jurchens (女真), the Yellow River (黄河) burst its dykes at Yangwu (阳武) and shifted its course south, into the Huai (淮) river system. Vast farmlands in the plains became swamp, and the river would not return north for over six centuries. Jin officials struggled to rebuild dykes while the empire also faced growing threats from the Mongol (蒙古) steppe. For the farmers there, it felt as if the world itself had moved.",
  "jurchens:wind": "Imagined: a blizzard wind howled across the forests of the upper Songhua (松花江) as Jurchen (女真) hunters returned from a winter sable hunt. They dug into the snow, pressed close to their horses and dogs, and waited out the storm under hides. One elder sang an old song to the spirit of the north wind. When the gale finally dropped, they counted each man, found all alive, and rode on to the village where smoke rose from the kang stoves.",
  "jurchens:fire": "In 1127 the Jurchen Jin (金) armies captured the Song (宋) capital Kaifeng (开封) in the Jingkang incident (靖康之变). They carried off the retired emperor Huizong (徽宗), the emperor Qinzong (钦宗), thousands of members of the court, and the imperial treasures and libraries, and parts of the great city burned. For the Jurchens (女真) it was a staggering triumph, barely a dozen years after their uprising; for the Chinese it became a byword for national humiliation.",
  "jurchens:thunder": "In 1232 the Mongols (蒙古) besieged the Jin (金) capital at Kaifeng (开封). The Jurchen (女真) defenders used a new weapon: the zhentianlei (震天雷), heaven-shaking thunder, iron shells packed with gunpowder, lowered from the walls on chains onto the Mongol sappers below. The roar, chroniclers wrote, could be heard for a hundred li, and men and armour were blown to pieces. The Jin dynasty fell two years later, but the thunder of that siege was remembered.",
  "jurchens:birth": "The Manchus (满洲), descendants of the Jurchens (女真), told how three heavenly maidens bathed in the lake Bulhuri (布勒瑚里) at the foot of Mount Bukuri (布库里山). A magpie dropped a red fruit on the youngest, Fekulen (佛库伦), who swallowed it and became pregnant. Her son Bukuri Yongson (布库里雍顺) was born able to speak, and became the ancestor of Nurhaci's (努尔哈赤) clan, which founded the Qing (清). The magpie was revered ever after, and the story was told at the birth of every new hope.",
  "jurchens:death": "A Song (宋) account of the Jin, the Da Jin Guozhi (大金国志), describes the old Jurchen (女真) funeral: the dead were buried without coffins, horses and goods were burned as offerings (烧饭), and the mourners slashed their foreheads with knives so that blood and tears ran down together, called sending blood tears. It was grief made visible, the loudest honor a warrior people could give. As the Jin adopted Chinese ways, many of these customs faded.",

  // ── Khitans ──
  "khitans:hunt": "The Liao (辽) emperors moved their court with the seasons in camps called nabo (捺钵). In spring at the lakes of Changchun (长春州), drummers flushed the swans, and the emperor loosed a gyrfalcon (海东青) to bring down the first one. The first swan was offered at the ancestral shrine and celebrated with the first-swan feast (头鹅宴), where nobles stuck its feathers in their hair. For the Khitans (契丹) the hunt was the state itself on horseback.",
  "khitans:battle": "In 979 the Song (宋) emperor Taizong (太宗) besieged Liao's (辽) southern capital, Yanjing (modern Beijing). The Khitan generals Yelu Xiuge (耶律休哥) and Yelu Xiezhen (耶律斜轸) rode to its rescue, and at the Gaoliang river (高梁河) they crushed the Song army. Taizong, wounded by an arrow, fled south in a donkey cart. For the Khitans (契丹) it was a famous victory; the Sixteen Prefectures (燕云十六州) would remain in their hands, and the Song never took them back.",
  "khitans:gather": "In 1112 the Liao (辽) emperor Tianzuo (天祚帝) held his spring camp on the Songhua (松花江) river, where the chiefs gathered for the first-fish feast (头鱼宴): fish speared through holes in the ice. When the wine was flowing, he ordered the Jurchen (女真) chieftains to dance. One by one they did, except for Wanyan Aguda (完颜阿骨打), who stood and stared. The emperor wanted to kill him but was dissuaded. Three years later Aguda founded the Jin (金), which destroyed the Liao.",
  "khitans:ritual": "The Khitans (契丹) told that a heavenly man riding a white horse and a heavenly woman driving a grey ox met at Mount Muye (木叶山), where two rivers join, and their eight sons became the eight Khitan tribes. At Muye the Liao (辽) emperors sacrificed a white horse and a grey ox to heaven and earth, and to the ancestors, in the mountain-worship rite. The emperor asked the spirits for victories, good pasture and many children.",
  "khitans:rain": "In times of drought the Liao (辽) court held the sese rite (瑟瑟仪) to pray for rain. Willow branches were set up in the ground, and the emperor and nobles rode by shooting at them with arrows. Those who missed were punished by having to give wine to those who hit. If rain fell within three days, the officials received rewards. It was a horseman's prayer: drawing a bow to call down water on the grass.",
  "khitans:wind": "Imagined: a spring dust storm rolled out of the Gobi (戈壁) over the Liao (辽) camps. The yellow wind darkened the sky at noon; the court's felt tents shook and the herds turned their backs to the gale. Khitan (契丹) herders tied the young lambs inside the gers and waited, cloths over their mouths. When it passed, they walked the pastures to find the scattered sheep, and the shamans burned juniper to cleanse the camp.",
  "khitans:fire": "Imagined: at the Liao (辽) winter camp a lamp toppled in the night and fire ran through the felt tents. The Khitan guards beat at it with hides and drove the horses clear while families dragged out their children and saddles. At dawn the shaman scattered ash and spoke to the spirit of fire, which Khitans (契丹) honored with offerings of food burned at the hearth, asking it to stay inside the stove where it belonged.",
  "khitans:thunder": "Imagined: on the open grasslands of the Liao (辽), summer thunderstorms rolled in fast and lightning had nowhere to strike but horses and riders. When a storm approached, Khitan (契丹) herders galloped the herds into low ground and lay flat. A horse killed by lightning was left where it fell as belonging to heaven. Afterward the families offered a white sheep to the sky and thanked it for sparing them.",
  "khitans:birth": "The History of Liao (辽史) says that before Yelu Abaoji (耶律阿保机) was born in 872, his mother dreamed that the sun fell into her bosom. At his birth the tent was filled with a strange light and fragrance, and his body was like a three-year-old's; he could crawl at once. His grandmother feared for him in a time of clan feuds and hid him, rubbing his face with soot. He grew up to unite the Khitans (契丹) and found the Liao dynasty in 907.",
  "khitans:death": "When Abaoji (阿保机) died in 926, the custom was for loyal followers to follow him in death. His widow, Empress Shulu Ping (述律平), sent many officials to the tomb. One of them, Zhao Siwen (赵思温), replied: no one was closer to the emperor than you. She answered that her children were young and the state needed her, then cut off her own right hand and had it buried with him. She was called the Empress of the Severed Wrist (断腕). Older Khitan custom exposed the dead on mountain trees.",

  // ── Chinese: siege … wedding ──
  "chinese-middle:siege": "In 757, during the An Lushan (安禄山) rebellion, Zhang Xun (张巡) and Xu Yuan (许远) held the small walled city of Suiyang (睢阳) for ten months against rebel armies many times their size. When the grain ran out, the defenders ate tea leaves, paper, tree bark, then sparrows and rats netted on the walls. Every day fewer men could lift a bow. The city fell only three days before relief arrived, but it had shielded the rich lands of the Huai (淮) valley, and the people there remembered Zhang Xun in temples for centuries.",
  "chinese-middle:triumph": "In 620 the young Li Shimin (李世民), Prince of Qin, crushed the rebel Liu Wuzhou (刘武周) in the north and rode home. His soldiers made up a song on the march, the Prince of Qin Breaks the Ranks (秦王破阵乐), and the crowds in Chang'an (长安) sang it as the army entered the city. Later, as emperor, he turned it into a great court dance for 120 dancers in armour, with drums that could be heard a hundred li away. Even the Tang court's foreign guests asked to hear it.",
  "chinese:build": "In the 1570s the general Qi Jiguang (戚继光) rebuilt the Great Wall (长城) north of Beijing (北京) in brick, with hollow watchtowers every few hundred paces. Soldiers, convicts and hired labourers carried bricks up ridges so steep that, a local legend says, goats were sent up with bricks tied to their horns. On the flat stretches men pounded rammed earth with heavy stone tampers, singing the tamping chant (打夯号子) so that all the blows fell together: one leader calls, the whole crew answers, and the earth goes down another finger.",
  "chinese:voyage": "In 1405 Zheng He (郑和) sailed from Liujiagang (刘家港) near Suzhou (苏州) with more than two hundred ships and about 27,000 men, bound for Champa, Java, Ceylon and Calicut. On six more voyages over 28 years the fleet reached Hormuz and the coast of Africa. Before the last voyage, in 1431, the crews set up a stone at Changle (长乐) in Fujian (福建), thanking the sea goddess Tianfei (天妃, Mazu) for the times her lantern had appeared at the masthead in a storm and the sea had calmed.",
  "chinese-middle:festival": "After the Song capital Kaifeng (开封) was lost in 1127, Meng Yuanlao (孟元老) wrote down his memories of it in the Dream of Splendour in the Eastern Capital (东京梦华录). At the Lantern Festival (元宵) on the fifteenth of the first month, he recalled, a mountain of lanterns rose before the palace gate, acrobats, puppeteers and storytellers performed in the streets, and the emperor handed out wine to the crowd. Old men wept over the book in the south, remembering a city they would never see again.",
  "chinese-middle:games": "Cuju (蹴鞠), kicking a leather ball stuffed with feathers, was loved from the Han to the Ming (明). In Song times there were ball clubs with their own rules and famous players. The story goes that a clerk named Gao Qiu (高俅), sent on an errand to Prince Duan (端王), saw the ball fly his way and returned it with such a graceful kick that the prince kept him. The prince became Emperor Huizong (徽宗), and Gao Qiu rose to command the palace armies, all for one good kick.",
  "chinese:drought": "From 1637 to 1641 the rains failed again and again across the northern plains under the last Ming (明) emperor. Locusts followed the drought. Gazetteers from Henan (河南) and Shandong (山东) record that people ate grass roots, bark, and finally the white clay called Guanyin earth (观音土), which filled the belly and killed slowly. Whole villages walked away down the roads, carrying children and seed grain they could not plant. Many who lived through it never spoke of it again.",
  "chinese:quake": "Before dawn on 23 January 1556, the Jiajing (嘉靖) earthquake struck Shaanxi (陕西) and Shanxi (山西). In the loess country most families lived in cave homes (窑洞) dug into the soft hills, and the caves collapsed on them in their sleep; perhaps 830,000 people died, the deadliest earthquake ever recorded. A scholar who survived, Qin Keda (秦可大), wrote down advice for the future: at the first tremor do not rush outside, crouch and wait. His notes were passed on for centuries in the region.",
  "chinese-middle:winter": "Around 809 the poet Bai Juyi (白居易) wrote The Old Charcoal Seller (卖炭翁). An old man burns charcoal in the Zhongnan (终南) mountains, his face grey with smoke. His coat is thin, yet he wishes for colder weather, so his charcoal will sell. After a night of snow he drives his ox cart into Chang'an (长安) before dawn, and two palace envoys take the whole load for a length of red silk. Readers in every century have felt his cold hands and his empty cart.",
  "chinese:plague": "In the winter of 1910 pneumonic plague broke out in Harbin (哈尔滨) and spread along the railway with workers heading home for the New Year. The young doctor Wu Lien-teh (伍连德) found the plague passed from breath to breath. He had people wear gauze masks, set up quarantine in railway carriages, and, against deep custom, obtained permission to cremate thousands of frozen bodies. About 60,000 died, but by spring the epidemic was over. Families wept that their dead had no graves; others were alive because of it.",
  "chinese:wedding": "Imagined: on a lucky day chosen from the almanac, a Qing-era village heard the suona horns and gongs approaching. The groom's party carried a red sedan chair (花轿) to fetch the bride, who wore a red veil and had wept the ritual leaving songs with her mother the night before. At the groom's house the couple bowed to heaven and earth, to his parents, and to each other. That night friends crowded into the bridal room to tease them (闹洞房) until the candles burned low.",

  // ── Shang: siege … wedding ──
  "shang:siege": "Imagined: when the Guifang (鬼方) raiders came out of the west, the people of a Shang (商) border town crowded inside its rammed-earth walls with their pigs and grain jars. The walls were thick enough to drive a cart along the top. Each night the diviner burned a bone and read the cracks: will help come from the king? Will there be disaster? The grain shrank, the wells grew muddy, the women ground the last millet. On the ninth day the chariots of the king's army appeared on the plain.",
  "shang:triumph": "Oracle bones record that Lady Fu Hao (妇好), a consort of King Wu Ding (武丁), led armies against the Tufang (土方) and the Qiang (羌), once with some 13,000 soldiers, the largest force named on the bones. When the army came home, captives and spoils were offered to the royal ancestors in the temple, with wine poured from bronze vessels and great drums beaten. When she died, Wu Ding buried her with bronze axes, hundreds of jades and her own name cast on her vessels.",
  "shang:build": "Around 1600 BC the Shang (商) raised the walls of their city at Zhengzhou (郑州): nearly seven kilometres of rammed earth, in places twenty metres thick at the base. Imagined: thousands of farmers worked in the dry season between harvests. They set up wooden boards, poured in loess a few fingers deep, and pounded it hard with bundled wooden rams, layer after layer, the thud of the rams keeping time with a chant. Each layer was a hand's breadth; the wall grew a little each day.",
  "shang:voyage": "The Book of Documents (尚书) preserves the speeches of King Pan Geng (盘庚), who around 1300 BC moved the Shang (商) people to Yin (殷), near today's Anyang (安阳). The people did not want to leave their old city and grumbled loudly. Pan Geng scolded and coaxed them: the ancestors had moved many times before, he said, and a new home would bring new life, like shoots from a fallen tree. They went, carrying their bronzes and their grain, and Yin remained the capital for the rest of the dynasty.",
  "shang:festival": "The late Shang (商) kings followed the zhouji (周祭), a cycle of five kinds of offering made in turn to every royal ancestor, by name, on the day of the ten-day week that matched that ancestor's name. One full cycle took about 360 days, so the ritual itself counted the year. Imagined: when a new cycle began, the drums sounded, wine was poured from bronze jue cups, and the Shang, famous for their love of drink, feasted late into the night.",
  "shang:games": "Imagined: on the fields outside Yin (殷), young Shang (商) nobles practised for war and the hunt. Charioteers raced two-horse chariots over the packed earth, the spoked wheels rattling, the driver leaning into the turns while the archer beside him shot at a target. Oracle bones note that the king asked whether his chariot would meet misfortune; once, the bones record, a chariot overturned on a hunt and a prince was thrown. The crowd cheered the boldest drivers, and the king watched.",
  "shang:drought": "Later Chinese told that after the founder of Shang (商), Tang (成汤), took power, it did not rain for seven years. The diviners said a man must be sacrificed. Tang said: if a man must be offered, let it be me. He cut his hair and his nails, bathed, and went to the Mulberry Grove (桑林) to offer himself, blaming his own faults rather than the people's. Before the fire was lit, the story ends, the rain came down in torrents over the whole land.",
  "shang:quake": "Imagined: in the night the ground beneath Yin (殷) shook. The rammed-earth walls of the halls cracked, roof beams fell, and the people ran out into the dark. In the morning the diviners heated a turtle shell and asked the ancestors: is it the high god Di (帝) who is displeased? Is it an ancestor demanding offerings? Cattle were killed and wine poured. The people rebuilt the houses on the same foundations, as their ancestors had done, and listened to the earth for many nights.",
  "shang:winter": "Imagined: oracle bones ask about snow (雪) and cold winds, and in one cold winter at Yin (殷) the diviner asked again and again whether the snow would stop. The rivers froze, the millet stores were counted, and families packed close around the hearth in their half-buried houses. The men went out to hunt deer in the snow. The king offered to the river and the mountain spirits so that spring would come, and the people waited, listening for the ice to crack.",
  "shang:plague": "The oracle bones are full of illness: the king's tooth, his eyes, his belly, and many questions about whether a sickness was sent by an angry ancestor. Some inscriptions ask whether there will be a year of sickness (疾年). Imagined: when a fever spread through Yin (殷), family after family took to their mats. The diviners named the ancestor at fault, the king made offerings to calm him, and the people waited to learn whether the cracks had told the truth.",
  "shang:wedding": "Imagined: a Shang (商) king took brides from many allied clans, and the oracle bones even ask which clan's woman to take. Here a bride came from the Fang lands with her retinue, oxen and bronzes. She was led to the ancestral temple at Yin (殷), where the diviner had chosen the day and the ancestors were told of the union. Drums, bronze bells and ocarina-like xun played; wine flowed; and her clan and the king's were joined by her coming.",

  // ── Shu: siege … wedding ──
  "shu:siege": "In 263 the Wei (魏) general Zhong Hui (钟会) marched on Shu (蜀) with a huge army. Jiang Wei (姜维) pulled the Shu soldiers back into the Sword Gate pass (剑阁), a cleft in the cliffs where, as Li Bai (李白) later wrote, one man can hold off ten thousand. They held through the autumn, short of food, watching the Wei camp fires below. The pass never fell. But another Wei army crossed the mountains by a secret path, and Chengdu (成都) surrendered behind them. The soldiers at the pass, the chronicles say, smashed their swords on the rocks in grief.",
  "shu:triumph": "In 225 Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) led the Shu (蜀) army south into the hills of Nanzhong (南中). Later stories tell that he captured the chieftain Meng Huo (孟获) seven times and let him go seven times (七擒七纵), until Meng Huo vowed never to rebel again. When the army returned to Chengdu (成都) in the winter, the people came out to greet them, and the south was quiet. Local legend credits the campaign with the first mantou buns, made to replace an offering of human heads to the river spirits.",
  "shu:build": "Shu (蜀) lay behind the Qinling (秦岭) mountains, and its armies depended on the plank roads (栈道): beams driven into holes cut in sheer cliff faces, with boards laid on top, hanging over the river gorges. Shu workers repaired and rebuilt them again and again. To carry grain over them Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) had wooden carts built, the wooden ox and gliding horse (木牛流马). Imagined: the carpenters hung from ropes, chanting together as they swung the heavy beams into place.",
  "shu:voyage": "In 208, as Cao Cao's (曹操) army came south, Liu Bei (刘备) left Fancheng (樊城). More than a hundred thousand people chose to follow him, with thousands of carts of belongings, moving only a few miles a day. His officers urged him to leave them and run. He refused: to do great things, he said, one must have the people as the foundation; how can I abandon those who follow me? At Changban (长坂) the enemy cavalry caught them, and the people scattered. Many still reached the south.",
  "shu:festival": "Imagined: in Chengdu (成都), the Brocade City (锦官城), New Year came with the sound of bamboo stalks crackling in the fire to scare away evil spirits. Families hung peach-wood charms at their gates and drank pepper wine, the youngest first, since they were gaining a year, and the oldest last. In the markets weavers showed off new Shu (蜀) brocade, and children ran among the stalls with sweets made from Sichuan (四川) sugarcane.",
  "shu:games": "In 244, when Wei (魏) armies attacked Hanzhong (汉中), the Shu (蜀) minister Fei Yi (费祎) prepared to march to its relief. The scholar Lai Min (来敏) came to say farewell and asked for a game of weiqi (围棋). The army was armed and the horses saddled, yet Fei Yi played on calmly, absorbed in the game. Lai Min said: I was only testing you; a man this calm will surely defeat the enemy. Fei Yi marched, and the Wei army withdrew.",
  "shu:drought": "Imagined: the Dujiangyan (都江堰) weir kept the Chengdu (成都) plain green, but in the hill country of Shu (蜀) the summer rains failed. The rice terraces cracked and the wells sank. Farmers carried water up the slopes in buckets on shoulder poles, and the women prayed at the shrine of Li Bing (李冰), the old engineer who had tamed the Min (岷) river. The magistrate opened the granary; the people waited, eyes on the clouds over the western mountains.",
  "shu:quake": "Imagined: Shu (蜀) lies among some of the most restless mountains in the land. One spring the earth shook, and in the gorges whole hillsides slid into the rivers, carrying away stretches of the plank roads to the north. In the villages houses of timber and mud fell. The survivors camped in the open fields, afraid to go back under a roof, and the soldiers were sent out to dig. For months the grain carts could not pass.",
  "shu:winter": "Imagined: on the northern campaigns the Shu (蜀) soldiers crossed the Qinling (秦岭) into the Wei (魏) valley, and winter caught them in the mountains. Snow buried the plank roads; grain carts stopped; men wrapped their feet in straw and rags. Southern farmers who had never seen such cold huddled around fires of green wood. The army turned back, and Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) wrote that the realm's strength was being worn away. The soldiers only wanted to see the plain of Chengdu (成都) again.",
  "shu:plague": "In his Memorial on Sending Out the Troops (出师表), Zhuge Liang (诸葛亮) wrote: in the fifth month I crossed the Lu river and went deep into barren lands (五月渡泸，深入不毛). The southern rivers were feared for their miasma (瘴气), the fevers that struck soldiers from the north. Imagined: the men drank only boiled water, chewed herbs given by local guides, and buried their comrades on the riverbanks. Those who came home remembered the heat and the fever more than the fighting.",
  "shu:wedding": "In 209 Liu Bei (刘备) married the younger sister of Sun Quan (孙权), ruler of Wu (吴), to seal their alliance. The chronicles say she was strong-willed and fond of arms, and that more than a hundred of her maidservants stood around her with swords drawn. Each time Liu Bei entered her chambers, he felt a chill in his heart. It was a marriage of two houses rather than two hearts, and within a few years she returned to Wu.",

  // ── Wei: siege … wedding ──
  "wei:siege": "In the autumn of 219 Guan Yu (关羽) besieged Fancheng (樊城), held for Wei (魏) by Cao Ren (曹仁). Then the autumn rains flooded the Han river, and the water rose until only a few feet of the city wall stood above it. Parts of the wall crumbled, and the defenders were cut off from all help. Cao Ren drowned a white horse and swore to the garrison that he would hold or die with them. They fought from boats and the wall top, and held until relief came.",
  "wei:triumph": "In 207 Cao Cao (曹操) led his army across the northern wastes and defeated the Wuhuan (乌桓) at White Wolf Mountain (白狼山). On the march home, at Jieshi (碣石) by the Bohai Sea (渤海), he climbed the hill and wrote Viewing the Blue Sea (观沧海): the sun and moon seem to rise from its waves. He also wrote that though the tortoise lives long, it too must end, yet an old horse in the stable still dreams of a thousand li. The soldiers sang his verses all the way home.",
  "wei:build": "In 210 Cao Cao (曹操) built the Bronze Sparrow Terrace (铜雀台) at Ye (邺), a tall hall on a rammed-earth platform crowned with a great bronze bird. When it was done he climbed it with his sons and asked them to write rhapsodies. The young Cao Zhi (曹植) wrote his so quickly and so well that his father was astonished. For centuries afterward poets wrote of the terrace, and of the singing girls Cao Cao asked to perform there each month after his death.",
  "wei:voyage": "In the winter of 225 the Wei (魏) emperor Cao Pi (曹丕) sailed a great fleet down the canals to Guangling (广陵) to look across the Yangtze (长江) at Wu (吴). The river was high and wild, the far bank lined with walls and watchtowers. He looked out at the waves and sighed: Heaven has made this river to divide north from south. The waterways behind him began to freeze, and the fleet turned back. The soldiers were glad to go home without a crossing.",
  "wei:festival": "In the Wei (魏) court at Ye (邺), the Jian'an poets (建安七子) gathered with Cao Pi (曹丕) and Cao Zhi (曹植) for spring feasts in the West Garden (西园). On the Shangsi festival (上巳) in the third month, people went down to the rivers to wash away the ills of winter, and the poets floated wine cups on the stream; whoever the cup stopped by had to drink and write a poem. Cao Pi later recalled those days with longing: the feasts, the music, the friends in the moonlight.",
  "wei:games": "In his own book, the Dian Lun (典论), Cao Pi (曹丕) wrote that he loved fencing. At a feast, the general Deng Zhan (邓展) boasted of his skill, so Cao Pi challenged him with stalks of sugarcane as swords. He struck Deng Zhan's arm three times, and when Deng asked for another bout, Cao Pi hit him squarely on the forehead. The whole hall burst out laughing. Cao Pi also wrote proudly of his skill at tanqi (弹棋), a game of flicking pieces across a board.",
  "wei:drought": "In 194 Cao Cao (曹操) and Lü Bu (吕布) were fighting over Yan province when drought and locusts struck. The chronicles say a measure of grain cost five hundred thousand coins and that people ate one another. Both armies ran out of food and broke off the war. Cao Cao's soldiers learned what famine meant; soon after, he set up the military farms (屯田), where soldiers and refugees farmed abandoned land so the army would never starve like that again.",
  "wei:quake": "Imagined: one spring under Wei (魏), the earth shook at Luoyang (洛阳). Tiles fell from palace roofs, and in the wards of the rebuilt city mud-brick walls cracked. Officials hurried to the court with memorials: earthquakes, the scholars said, showed that the harmony of yin and yang was disturbed, and the emperor must examine his conduct. The common people simply swept up the broken tiles, propped up their walls with timber, and slept outside for a few nights.",
  "wei:winter": "In early 206 Cao Cao (曹操) led his army north through the Taihang (太行) mountains in winter to put down a rebellion, and wrote Bitter Cold (苦寒行). The poem says: the north wind howls, the snow falls thick, bears crouch in front of us, tigers roar beside the road. The men have no food, no shelter, no wood; they chop ice to cook their gruel. Even the general longs for home. Soldiers recognized their own march in his words.",
  "wei:plague": "In 217 a terrible epidemic swept northern China. Cao Zhi (曹植) wrote that in every house there was the pain of the dead, in every room the sound of weeping. Four of the seven Jian'an (建安) poets died in a single year. Cao Pi (曹丕) wrote to his friend Wu Zhi (吴质): last year the plague took so many of our friends. When I think how we rode and drank and wrote together, and now they are dust, my grief cannot be put into words.",
  "wei:wedding": "In 204, when Cao Cao's (曹操) army took Ye (邺), the young Cao Pi (曹丕) entered the house of the Yuan (袁) family. There, the story goes, he found Lady Zhen (甄氏), who hid her face in her mother-in-law's lap. When he lifted her face he was struck by her beauty, and he soon married her. She was famous for her grace and learning, and she became the mother of the future emperor Cao Rui (曹叡). Later poets imagined that Cao Zhi (曹植) too had loved her.",

  // ── Wu: siege … wedding ──
  "wu:siege": "In 222–223 a Wei (魏) army under Cao Zhen (曹真) surrounded Jiangling (江陵), held for Wu (吴) by Zhu Ran (朱然) with only about five thousand men. The enemy raised earth mounds and towers, shot arrows into the city like rain, and built a pontoon bridge to the river island. Then sickness spread inside the walls, and fewer than half the defenders could fight. Zhu Ran stayed calm, cheered his men, and found and executed a traitor. After six months the Wei army gave up and went home.",
  "wu:triumph": "After the fire at the Red Cliffs (赤壁) in 208, Zhou Yu (周瑜) came home a hero at thirty-four. He was a fine musician: people in Wu (吴) said that if a musician played a wrong note, even after three cups of wine, Zhou Lang would turn his head (曲有误，周郎顾). Imagined: at the victory feast the singers made a few mistakes on purpose, just so the handsome young general would look their way. The whole Wu court laughed, and the soldiers toasted the wind that had saved them.",
  "wu:build": "In 212 Sun Quan (孙权) rebuilt the old fort on the hill above the Yangtze (长江) into the Stone City (石头城), guarding his new capital at Jianye (建业). Workers cut and stacked red sandstone along the cliff where the river ran close. Imagined: boatmen ferried the blocks, and labourers hauled them up with ropes to the rhythm of a chant, the leader calling out and the crew answering together. Ships of the Wu (吴) navy anchored below its walls.",
  "wu:voyage": "In 230 Sun Quan (孙权) sent the generals Wei Wen (卫温) and Zhuge Zhi (诸葛直) with ten thousand soldiers across the sea to look for the islands called Yizhou (夷洲) and Danzhou (亶洲). They sailed east into open water, perhaps to Taiwan. After a year, sickness had killed eight or nine out of ten of the men. They came home with a few thousand islanders, and the court punished the generals. It was among the first recorded voyages from the mainland across that strait.",
  "wu:festival": "In the Wu (吴) lands of Jiangnan (江南) the Dragon Boat Festival on the fifth day of the fifth month was held for a local hero: Wu Zixu (伍子胥), whose body had been thrown into the river centuries before and who was said to ride the tide as a spirit. Imagined: villagers raced long painted boats to the beat of drums, threw rice wrapped in leaves into the water, and hung mugwort over their doors against the summer's poisons and sickness.",
  "wu:games": "Weiqi (围棋) was loved in Wu (吴). One old game record is said to come from a match between Sun Ce (孙策) and his officer Lü Fan (吕范), though historians doubt it. The scholars of Jianye (建业) played long games through the night by lamplight, and the Wu writer Wei Zhao (韦昭) even wrote an essay against the game, complaining that young men wasted their days on it. Imagined: soldiers in camp scratched grids on the ground and played with pebbles.",
  "wu:drought": "Imagined: in a dry year in the lower Yangtze (长江), the rice fields of Wu (吴) cracked and the canals sank to mud. Farmers pedalled the wooden chain pumps (龙骨车) day and night, singing to keep their feet in time, to lift what water was left. The women burned incense to the river spirits and the dragon king. The granaries of Jianye (建业) were opened, and boats brought rice from upriver. When the rain finally came, children ran out to dance in it.",
  "wu:quake": "Imagined: in the Wu (吴) capital of Jianye (建业) one summer night, the ground shook. Bells in the halls rang by themselves, roof tiles slid off, and in the market lanes wooden houses swayed and some fell. People fled to the riverbank in the dark, crying out to the gods. In the morning the officials counted the damage and the diviners argued over what it meant. The boatmen said the river had leaped, and the old people prayed at the temple of the earth god.",
  "wu:winter": "In the winter of 224 the Wei (魏) emperor Cao Pi (曹丕) brought a great fleet to the north bank of the Yangtze (长江). The Wu (吴) general Xu Sheng (徐盛) had his men put up hundreds of li of false walls and towers of wood and reed along the south bank overnight. Cao Pi saw what looked like an endless fortress, and the river was rough and icy. The Wei fleet turned back. The Wu soldiers, shivering on the bank behind their reed walls, laughed in relief.",
  "wu:plague": "Imagined: in the humid summer at the Wu (吴) garrisons along the southern rivers, fever came through the barracks. Northern-born soldiers fell first, then the local men. The camp doctors boiled herbs in big pots, and the sick lay in rows under the eaves, fanned by their comrades. Each day more were carried out to the hills. Officers wrote to Jianye (建业) for fresh troops. Those who recovered went back to their posts thin and quiet.",
  "wu:wedding": "Around 199 Sun Ce (孙策) and his close friend Zhou Yu (周瑜) took the city of Wan (宛), and there married two sisters famous for their beauty, the elder and younger Qiao (大乔, 小乔). Sun Ce joked to Zhou Yu that even with the war, having the Qiao sisters as wives was happiness enough. Later poets loved the pair of weddings: the two young heroes, sworn friends, now brothers by marriage too. Within a year Sun Ce was dead.",

  // ── Zhu Xi's Legacy: siege … wedding ──
  "zhu-xis-legacy:siege": "From 1243 the Song people of Hechuan (合川) in Sichuan (四川) held the Diaoyu Fortress (钓鱼城), a walled mountain plateau between three rivers. Refugees farmed fields inside the walls and drew water from wells there, so they could hold out for years. In 1259 the Mongol ruler Möngke besieged it in person and died beneath its walls. The fortress held for some thirty-six years in all. When it finally opened its gates in 1279, it did so only on a promise that the people inside would be spared.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:triumph": "In 1161 a Jin army reached the Yangtze (长江) at Caishi (采石). The Song troops there had no commander, and the soldiers were sitting by the road in despair. Yu Yunwen (虞允文), a civil official sent only to reward the troops, gathered them and led the river fleet himself. The paddle-wheel warships drove the Jin back. When news of Caishi reached Jiangnan (江南), the people celebrated; a bookish scholar had saved them, and the teachers of the Way pointed to him as proof of a scholar's courage.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:build": "In 1179 Zhu Xi (朱熹), prefect of Nankang (南康), found the old White Deer Grotto Academy (白鹿洞书院) at the foot of Mount Lu (庐山) in ruins. He had it rebuilt with his own salary and gifts from friends, collected books, and wrote its rules: let the father and son have affection, the ruler and minister righteousness. Imagined: masons and carpenters worked under the pines, and students carried tiles between their lessons, reciting the classics as they climbed the path.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:voyage": "In 1167 Zhu Xi (朱熹) travelled more than a thousand li from Fujian (福建) to Changsha (长沙) to visit his friend Zhang Shi (张栻) at the Yuelu Academy (岳麓书院). They debated the nature of the mind for two months, sometimes three days and nights without sleep. Students flocked to hear them; local tradition says so many came on horseback that their horses drank the pond dry. Afterward the two climbed Mount Heng (衡山) together in the snow and wrote poems on the way.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:festival": "In his Family Rituals (家礼), Zhu Xi (朱熹) set out how a household should greet the New Year. Before dawn on the first day, the head of the family opens the ancestral shrine, lights incense, and offers wine and tea to the ancestors' tablets. Then the whole household bows to the elders in order, the young to the old. Imagined: after the rites, firecrackers burst in the lanes of a Fujian (福建) town, and the children ran out in new clothes to visit their uncles.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:games": "The Confucians kept an old community archery rite, the village archery (乡射礼), and Zhu Xi's (朱熹) followers revived it. Imagined: in an academy courtyard in Fujian (福建), two pairs of archers stepped up, bowed, and shot in turn to the music of the zither and drum. What mattered was not only hitting the target but keeping time, keeping calm, and bowing to the winner. The loser drank a cup of wine as a penalty, smiling, and everyone agreed the rite had made them better men.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:drought": "In 1181 drought and famine struck eastern Zhejiang (浙江), and Zhu Xi (朱熹) was sent to direct relief. He travelled from village to village, often on foot, inspecting granaries and checking that rice reached the hungry rather than the clerks. He wrote memorials to the court describing families eating grass and selling their children. He believed a scholar's learning meant nothing if it could not fill a starving person's bowl, and he worked himself to exhaustion that winter.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:quake": "Imagined: one night in a Fujian (福建) valley, the earth rumbled and an academy's lecture hall shook, sending scrolls and inkstones to the floor. The students rushed out into the courtyard. In the morning their teacher, a follower of Zhu Xi (朱熹), gathered them among the fallen tiles and spoke of reverence (敬): when heaven and earth move, the gentleman examines himself and keeps his heart steady. Then they rolled up their sleeves and helped the villagers rebuild.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:winter": "In 1093 the scholars Yang Shi (杨时) and You Zuo (游酢) went to visit their teacher Cheng Yi (程颐), whose learning Zhu Xi (朱熹) would later carry on. They found him sitting with his eyes closed in meditation, and did not dare to wake him. They stood waiting outside the door. When Cheng Yi finally opened his eyes, the snow outside was a foot deep. The story, Standing in the Snow at Cheng's Gate (程门立雪), became the model of a student's devotion.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:plague": "Imagined: when an epidemic swept a county in Fujian (福建), the local scholars who followed Zhu Xi (朱熹) met in their academy hall. They opened the community granary to the sick, bought herbs with their own money, and wrote out remedies on notices posted at crossroads. Some families fled; the scholars stayed, saying that benevolence (仁) is shown when neighbours suffer. They buried the dead with proper rites, so that even in disaster the ceremonies of the living did not break.",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:wedding": "Zhu Xi's Family Rituals laid out a simplified wedding that spread across China, Korea and Vietnam. The groom himself rides to fetch the bride (亲迎), presents a wild goose as a pledge of faithfulness, and bows to her parents. At his home the couple bow to each other and drink from the two halves of a split gourd (合卺), bound by a red cord: two made one. Imagined: the bride's mother wept at the gate as the procession left.",

  // ── Japanese: siege … wedding ──
  "japanese:siege": "In 1333 Kusunoki Masashige (楠木正成) held the little mountain fort of Chihaya (千早) on Mount Kongō (金剛山) with perhaps a thousand men against a huge Kamakura (鎌倉) army. The Taiheiki (太平記) tells how the defenders rolled logs and boulders down on the climbers, poured boiling water and burning oil, and dressed straw dummies in armour to draw the enemy into the open. When the besiegers tried to cut off their water, the fort had its own springs. Chihaya held for months, and the siege outlasted the Kamakura regime itself.",
  "japanese:triumph": "In 1274, when the Mongol fleet landed at Hakata (博多), a young warrior from Higo (肥後), Takezaki Suenaga (竹崎季長), charged ahead to claim the first strike. After the invaders withdrew he waited for a reward, but none came. So he rode all the way to Kamakura (鎌倉) to plead his case in person, and was granted land and a horse. Grateful, he had his deeds painted on a scroll, the Mōko Shūrai Ekotoba (蒙古襲来絵詞), which still shows him galloping through arrows and exploding bombs.",
  "japanese:build": "In 743 Emperor Shōmu (聖武天皇) vowed to cast a great bronze Buddha at Tōdai-ji (東大寺) in Nara (奈良), and asked that every person contribute even a blade of grass or a handful of earth. The monk Gyōki (行基), loved by the common people, gathered workers and donations from across the land. Some 2.6 million people are said to have helped. At the eye-opening ceremony in 752, an Indian monk painted in the pupils while musicians from as far as Tang China (唐) and Korea played gagaku.",
  "japanese-early:voyage": "In 804 the young monk Kūkai (空海) sailed with the embassy to Tang China (唐). A storm scattered the four ships; his drifted for more than a month and came ashore far south in Fujian, where officials took the ragged travellers for pirates. Kūkai wrote a letter in such elegant Chinese that the governor was astonished and let them go on to Chang'an (長安). Two of the four ships were lost. He came home with the esoteric teachings of Shingon (真言).",
  "japanese:festival": "Imagined: in an Edo (江戸) townhouse at the year's end, the neighbours gathered to pound mochi, one swinging the heavy mallet while another turned the steaming rice between blows, calling out in rhythm. Pine and bamboo kadomatsu stood by the gates. At midnight the temple bells rang 108 times to drive away the 108 worldly desires. On New Year's morning the family ate ozōni soup, the children got pocket money, and everyone set out for the first shrine visit.",
  "japanese:games": "In the 1780s and 1790s Edo (江戸) was mad for sumo. The great wrestler Tanikaze Kajinosuke (谷風梶之助) won 63 bouts in a row, and in 1789 he and Onogawa (小野川) were the first to be granted the white rope of yokozuna. Crowds packed the temple grounds for the charity tournaments (勧進相撲), woodblock prints of the wrestlers sold everywhere, and the drum on its tall tower called the city to the bouts each morning. Tanikaze died in an epidemic in 1795, still a champion.",
  "japanese-early:drought": "In 1181 drought, followed by the Yōwa (養和) famine, struck the capital Kyoto (京都). Kamo no Chōmei (鴨長明) wrote in his Hōjōki (方丈記) that the starving collapsed by the walls and along the river, and the smell of the dead filled the city. A priest named Ryūgyō (隆暁) went through the streets writing the sacred letter A on the foreheads of the dead, to bind them to the Buddha; in two months, Chōmei wrote, he counted more than 42,300 in the city alone.",
  "japanese:quake": "On the night of 11 November 1855 the Ansei (安政) earthquake struck Edo (江戸). Fires broke out across the low-lying merchant districts and thousands died. Within days, woodblock prints called namazu-e flooded the city: pictures of the giant catfish whose thrashing under the earth caused quakes, being beaten by angry townspeople, or pinned down by the god of Kashima (鹿島). Some prints showed carpenters and plasterers thanking the catfish for the rebuilding work. People laughed at them to bear their grief.",
  "japanese:winter": "In 1837 Suzuki Bokushi (鈴木牧之), a cloth merchant from Echigo (越後), published Snow Country Tales (北越雪譜). He wanted city people to understand life where the snow piled higher than the houses. In winter, he wrote, families dug tunnels from their doors, shovelled snow off roofs before the weight crushed them, and walked on snow shoes. Avalanches buried travellers; women wove the famous Echigo chijimi linen in the long dark months, then bleached it on the snow in spring.",
  "japanese:plague": "From 735 to 737 smallpox swept from Kyushu (九州) through all of Japan. Perhaps a quarter to a third of the people died. In the capital at Nara (奈良), all four Fujiwara (藤原) brothers, the most powerful men at court, died in a single year. Farmers abandoned their fields, and the court sent rice and medicine to the provinces. In his grief and fear Emperor Shōmu (聖武天皇) turned to the Buddha; temples were ordered in every province, and the Great Buddha (大仏) at Tōdai-ji (東大寺) followed.",
  "japanese-early:wedding": "Among the Heian (平安) nobility a marriage began with the young man's secret visits. He came to the lady's house at night and left before dawn, sending a morning-after poem. If he came three nights in a row, the marriage was sealed: on the third night her family served the couple small rice cakes, the third-night mochi (三日夜餅), and the next day held a feast to present him to her household. Poems and the sleeves of parting fill the literature of the age.",

  // ── Yamato: siege … wedding ──
  "yamato:siege": "In 587 the powerful Mononobe no Moriya (物部守屋), who opposed the new Buddhist faith, fortified his estate at Shibukawa (渋川) with rice-stalk palisades and held out against the Soga (蘇我). His archers shot from the trees, and the attackers fell back three times. The fourteen-year-old Prince Umayado (厩戸皇子), later called Shōtoku (聖徳), carved images of the Four Heavenly Kings (四天王) and vowed to build them a temple if they won. Moriya was shot from his tree, the stronghold fell, and Shitennō-ji (四天王寺) was built in Naniwa (難波).",
  "yamato:triumph": "Imagined: in the age of the great tombs, warriors returned to their village in the Yamato (大和) basin from a campaign in the west. They wore iron helmets and short armour of iron plates, like the haniwa soldiers set on the tomb mounds. The women ran out to meet them; a shaman shook a bell-hung mirror; the elders pounded rice for the feast. Around the fire the men told of the sea crossing, and drummers and singers turned their deeds into songs.",
  "yamato:build": "The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) tells that Emperor Nintoku (仁徳天皇) climbed a tower and saw no smoke rising from the people's kitchens, so he cancelled all taxes for three years, though his own palace roof leaked. When smoke rose again, the people came gladly to build. The great keyhole mound named after him, the Daisen (大仙) kofun near Sakai (堺), is nearly half a kilometre long. Imagined: thousands of labourers carried baskets of earth, singing to keep pace, as the moats filled with water.",
  "yamato:voyage": "In 607 Ono no Imoko (小野妹子) sailed from Naniwa (難波) to the Sui court in China, carrying a letter that began: the Son of Heaven where the sun rises, to the Son of Heaven where the sun sets. The emperor Yang was displeased, but sent an envoy back with him. The voyage was dangerous; ships went by way of the Korean coast, waiting for the right winds. The envoys and students who followed brought home Buddhism, writing and law.",
  "yamato:festival": "At the Niiname-sai (新嘗祭) in the eleventh month, the ruler of Yamato (大和) offered the first rice of the harvest to the gods and ate it with them, renewing the bond between heaven and the rice fields. In the villages, the old provincial records say, young men and women went up to mountains like Tsukuba (筑波) for the utagaki (歌垣), festivals where they sang answering songs to one another through the night, flirting in verse.",
  "yamato:games": "The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) tells that in the reign of Emperor Suinin (垂仁天皇), a strongman named Taima no Kehaya (当麻蹶速) boasted that no one in the land could match him. Nomi no Sukune (野見宿禰) was summoned from Izumo (出雲), and the two fought before the emperor, kicking at each other, until Sukune broke Kehaya's ribs and back. It is remembered as the origin of sumo. Sukune later suggested replacing human sacrifices at royal tombs with clay haniwa figures.",
  "yamato:drought": "In 642, the Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) records, the summer drought was long. Village priests sacrificed horses and cattle and moved the markets as the old customs taught; then the minister Soga no Emishi (蘇我蝦夷) had sutras read in the temples, and only a little rain fell. At last Empress Kōgyoku (皇極天皇) went to the source of the Minabuchi (南淵) river, knelt, bowed to the four directions and looked up to heaven. Thunder came, the record says, and it rained for five days.",
  "yamato:quake": "In 684, the Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) records, a great earthquake shook the whole country, the earliest Nankai (南海) quake on record. Men and women cried out and fled in confusion; mountains collapsed and rivers surged. Temples, shrines and storehouses were destroyed. In Tosa more than five hundred thousand shiro of rice fields sank beneath the sea. The old men of Izu (伊豆) said they had never seen such a thing, and a tsunami struck the coast.",
  "yamato:winter": "Imagined: in a hard winter in the Yamato (大和) basin, the snow lay deep on the hills and the stored rice ran short. Families huddled around the hearths of their pit-dwellings, burning brushwood and wrapping themselves in hemp and fur. Men went into the mountains to hunt deer and boar; women dug roots under the snow. At the solstice the shaman held a rite to call the sun back, and the elders sang the old songs until spring came.",
  "yamato:plague": "The Nihon Shoki (日本書紀) tells that in the reign of Emperor Sujin (崇神天皇) a great epidemic killed more than half the people, and many others fled their homes. The emperor fasted and prayed. In a dream the god Ōmononushi of Mount Miwa (三輪山) appeared and said the sickness was his doing, and that if a man named Ōtataneko (大田田根子) were made his priest, the land would be at peace. Ōtataneko was found, the god was worshipped, and the plague ended.",
  "yamato:wedding": "The Kojiki (古事記) tells how the gods Izanagi (伊邪那岐) and Izanami (伊邪那美) made the first marriage. They walked in opposite directions around the heavenly pillar and met. Izanami spoke first: what a fine young man! But the child born of that union was a failure, because the woman had spoken first. They circled again; this time Izanagi spoke first, and from that marriage were born the islands of Japan. The Yamato (大和) told it as the pattern for all weddings.",

  // ── Sengoku Daimyo: siege … wedding ──
  "sengoku-daimyo:siege": "In 1575 Takeda Katsuyori (武田勝頼) besieged Nagashino Castle (長篠城) with fifteen thousand men; inside were about five hundred, nearly out of food. A foot soldier, Torii Sune'emon (鳥居強右衛門), slipped out at night, swam the river, and reached Tokugawa Ieyasu (徳川家康), who promised relief. On his way back he was captured. The Takeda told him to shout to the castle that no help was coming. Instead he cried out: help is near, hold on! He was killed beneath the walls, and the castle held.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:triumph": "In 1560 Oda Nobunaga (織田信長), a young lord with a few thousand men, struck the huge army of Imagawa Yoshimoto (今川義元) in a sudden storm at Okehazama (桶狭間), and Yoshimoto was killed. Before setting out Nobunaga had danced and sung from the play Atsumori (敦盛): man's fifty years are a dream. After the victory he gave thanks at Atsuta Shrine (熱田神宮) and built a roofed earthen wall there, still called the Nobunaga Wall (信長塀). The townspeople of Owari (尾張) celebrated their lord who had beaten the odds.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:build": "From 1576 Oda Nobunaga (織田信長) built Azuchi Castle (安土城) on a hill above Lake Biwa (琵琶湖), with a seven-storey tower painted in gold and lacquer. One huge stone, the Jagaishi (蛇石), was dragged up the hill by some ten thousand men over three days. According to Ōta Gyūichi's (太田牛一) chronicle, they hauled it to the sound of flutes, drums and singing to keep the ropes pulling together. The stone slipped once and crushed more than a hundred men.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:voyage": "In 1582 four Japanese boys, Christian students in Kyushu (九州), sailed from Nagasaki (長崎) for Europe: the Tenshō (天正) embassy. They crossed to Macao, Goa and around Africa, reaching Lisbon in 1584 and Rome in 1585, where the Pope received them and the crowds marvelled at their kimono. They learned to play the harpsichord, lute and harp. When they returned in 1590 they played Western music for Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉), who asked to hear it three times.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:festival": "In the spring of 1598 Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉) held a great cherry-blossom party at Daigo-ji (醍醐の花見) in Kyoto (京都). He had seven hundred cherry trees planted, and some 1,300 guests, mostly women of his household, came dressed in new kimono, changing clothes twice during the day. There were tea houses along the paths, poems tied to the branches, and music under the blossoms. Hideyoshi was old and ill; he died that summer, and the party became his farewell.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:games": "Oda Nobunaga (織田信長) loved sumo. In 1578, Ōta Gyūichi (太田牛一) records, he gathered 1,500 wrestlers from all the provinces at Azuchi Castle (安土城) and watched them wrestle from morning until evening. The winners were rewarded with swords, silver and even places as retainers in his household. Nobunaga's officials marked out a ring and judged the bouts, and some say this helped shape the round ring of later sumo. The crowd's cheers echoed up the castle hill.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:drought": "Imagined: in a dry summer in the Sengoku (戦国) years, the irrigation ponds of a village in Ōmi sank to cracked mud. Villages upstream and downstream had fought over water before, sometimes with spears. This time the elders met at the shrine and agreed to share it by the clock of a burning incense stick (番水): each village opened its gates for the time one stick burned. Then they sang a rain dance, beating drums through the night.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:quake": "In January 1586 the Tenshō (天正) earthquake shook central Japan. Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉), at Sakamoto (坂本) on Lake Biwa (琵琶湖), dropped everything and fled to Osaka (大坂). In the mountains of Hida (飛騨), a landslide buried Kaerikumo Castle (帰雲城) and the town beneath it; the whole Uchigashima (内ヶ島) clan, gathered to celebrate a peace, vanished in a night. Legends of their buried gold still circulate. Along the coast of Wakasa Bay (若狭湾), a tsunami swept away fishing villages.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:winter": "In the winter of 1584 the warlord Sassa Narimasa (佐々成政) crossed the Northern Alps in deep snow, over the Tateyama (立山) range by the Zara pass, to reach Tokugawa Ieyasu (徳川家康) in Hamamatsu (浜松). It was a desperate journey; many of his men are said to have died of cold. In the end Ieyasu had already made peace, and the crossing was in vain. The story, called the Sarasara-goe (さらさら越え), is remembered in Toyama (富山) as a tale of stubborn courage against the mountains.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:plague": "Smallpox came again and again in the Sengoku (戦国) years. One child it struck, around 1572, was Date Bontenmaru (伊達梵天丸), heir of the Date clan; the fever took the sight of his right eye. His mother, the story says, turned cold toward her disfigured son. He grew up to be Date Masamune (伊達政宗), the One-Eyed Dragon (独眼竜), lord of Sendai (仙台). Families everywhere hung red charms and painted red figures of the warrior Tametomo (為朝) to frighten the smallpox god away.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:wedding": "Around 1561 a poor foot soldier named Tōkichirō (藤吉郎), later Toyotomi Hideyoshi (豊臣秀吉), married Nene (ねね), a girl of samurai family, against her mother's wishes. Nene later recalled that the wedding was held in his small row house, with straw mats spread on the earthen floor and only the simplest food and sake. Their marriage of love lasted through his rise to rule the realm. She remained his closest partner and adviser, and he wrote her affectionate letters from every campaign.",

  // ── Koreans: siege … wedding ──
  "koreans:siege": "In the winter of 1636 King Injo (인조) and his court fled to the mountain fortress of Namhansanseong (남한산성) as the Qing army swept south. Some thirteen thousand defenders held its walls for forty-seven days in bitter cold. Food ran low; soldiers' hands froze to their weapons; horses were slaughtered for meat. Inside, the ministers argued bitterly between holding out and making peace. In the end the king walked down to Samjeondo (삼전도) to submit, and the soldiers wept to see it.",
  "koreans-early:triumph": "In 1019 the Goryeo (고려) general Gang Gam-chan (강감찬) destroyed a Khitan army at Gwiju (귀주); the chronicles say only a few thousand of a hundred thousand escaped. When the old general, past seventy, brought the army home, King Hyeonjong (현종) came out to meet him and with his own hands put eight golden flowers in Gang's hair. The people of Gaegyeong (개경) lined the road to cheer the small, plain-looking scholar-general who had saved them.",
  "koreans:build": "From 1794 to 1796 King Jeongjo (정조) built the fortress of Hwaseong (화성) at Suwon (수원). The scholar Jeong Yak-yong (정약용) designed a lifting crane, the geojunggi (거중기), with pulleys so that a few men could raise heavy stones. Unusually for the time, the labourers were paid wages, and when the summer heat came the king sent them medicine. Every worker's name and pay was recorded in the Hwaseong Seongyeok Uigwe (화성성역의궤), with drawings of each tool.",
  "koreans:voyage": "In 1488 the scholar-official Choe Bu (최부) was sailing home from Jeju (제주) to mourn his father when a storm drove his boat out to sea. For fourteen days the forty-three men drifted, out of water, drinking rain and their own urine. They washed up on the coast of Zhejiang, were taken for pirates, and then escorted all the way to Beijing and home overland. Every man survived. Choe Bu wrote it all down in his Record of Drifting Across the Sea (표해록).",
  "koreans:festival": "At Chuseok (추석), the harvest full moon of the eighth month, families went home to bow at their ancestors' graves, clear the grass from the mounds, and offer the new rice and songpyeon (송편) rice cakes steamed on pine needles. On the south coast that night, women joined hands in a great circle under the moon and danced the ganggangsullae (강강술래), one woman singing a line and all answering, faster and faster until dawn.",
  "koreans:games": "On Dano (단오), the fifth day of the fifth month, villagers crowded around a sand ring to watch ssireum (씨름) wrestling, two men gripping each other's thigh sashes. The prize was a bull. Around 1780 the painter Kim Hong-do (김홍도) painted a ssireum match: two wrestlers locked together, the crowd sitting around them, hats off, leaning in, and a boy selling taffy (엿) who has not even looked up. Girls, meanwhile, swung high on the swings (그네) in their best clothes.",
  "koreans:drought": "In 1670 and 1671 drought, cold and floods ruined harvest after harvest across Joseon (조선): the Gyeongsin great famine (경신대기근). Locusts and epidemics followed. Perhaps a million people died. The court cut its own meals and opened the granaries, and officials ran gruel stations where the starving lined up by the thousands. Records tell of mothers abandoning children by the road. Old people later counted their lives from that famine, as the year when everyone went hungry.",
  "koreans-early:quake": "In 779, the Samguk Sagi (삼국사기) records, a great earthquake struck the Silla (신라) capital of Gyeongju (경주). Houses collapsed and more than a hundred people were killed. Temples, palaces and the lanes of the golden city cracked. The monks of the great temples chanted sutras to calm the earth, and the king opened the granaries. The people of Gyeongju rebuilt in stone and timber, and the event stayed in the chronicles for a thousand years.",
  "koreans:winter": "Each year as winter came, the women of every Korean village gathered for kimjang (김장), making enough kimchi (김치) to last until spring. They salted hundreds of cabbages, rubbed in red pepper, garlic and salted fish, and packed it into great earthenware jars (옹기) buried in the yard so they would not freeze. Neighbours worked house by house, sharing food and gossip. Inside, the ondol (온돌) floors were heated from the kitchen fire, and families slept close on the warm stones.",
  "koreans:plague": "In 1821 cholera reached Joseon (조선) for the first time, entering through Pyongyang (평양). The sickness came with cramps that twisted the legs, and people said a rat spirit was climbing up inside the body; they called it the rat sickness (쥐통). Families pasted pictures of cats on their gates and rubbed cat skins on the sick. Within weeks the dead were counted in the tens of thousands. The king ordered rites to the spirits of the land and opened the medicine offices.",
  "koreans:wedding": "In a Joseon (조선) wedding the groom rode to the bride's house and presented a wooden wild goose (기러기) to her mother, a pledge to be as faithful as geese, which mate for life. In the courtyard the bride, her cheeks marked with red dots (연지곤지), bowed to him and he bowed back, across a table laden with chestnuts, dates, a rooster and a hen. They shared wine from the two halves of one gourd. Then the groom's friends would tease him by beating his feet.",

  // ── Choson (Gojoseon): siege … wedding ──
  "choson:siege": "In 109 BC two Han armies closed around Wanggeom (王險城, 왕검성), the walled capital of Wiman Joseon (위만조선). For nearly a year the city held. Imagined: inside the walls the grain pits emptied, the people ate horses and boiled leather, and the soldiers on the walls watched the enemy camps. Ministers argued over whether to surrender. In 108 BC some of them killed King Ugeo (우거왕), and still the minister Seonggi (성기) held out until he too was killed. Then the city opened its gates.",
  "choson:triumph": "Imagined: in the Bronze Age of Gojoseon (고조선), the warriors came home from a fight in the river valleys with captured horses and bronze. The villagers ran to meet them at the dolmen field. A shaman in a bronze mirror and bells danced and gave thanks to heaven, the young men's faces were painted, and the people drank millet wine and sang all night to the drum. Those who had fallen were buried under stones, and their names were sung too.",
  "choson:build": "Imagined: in a workshop of Gojoseon (고조선), bronze smiths cast the lute-shaped daggers (비파형 동검) that mark its lands. They carved moulds in soft stone, melted copper and tin over a charcoal fire blown with bellows, and poured the glowing metal while chanting to the fire spirit. Outside, villagers raised a new earthen rampart, pounding the soil with rams in time with a work song. The finished daggers were handed to the chief to be blessed.",
  "choson:voyage": "Around 195 BC, Chinese records tell, a man named Wiman (위만) fled east from the troubles in Yan with about a thousand followers. To show he came in peace, he tied his hair in a topknot and wore the local dress. He crossed the Pae river (浿水, 패수) and was given land on the frontier by King Jun (준왕) of Gojoseon (고조선) to guard it. Imagined: the refugees waded the river with their children, looking for the hills of their new home.",
  "choson:festival": "Imagined: when the sowing was done in the fifth month, the people of a Gojoseon (고조선) village gathered to honour the heavens. Later Chinese writers describe such festivals among their neighbours: they drank and danced day and night, dozens of people following each other in a line, stamping the ground and raising and lowering their hands in time. Here too the drum kept them dancing until the stars went out, and offerings were left for Dangun (단군), the founder.",
  "choson:games": "Imagined: on the riverbank below a Gojoseon (고조선) town, the young men gathered for contests after the harvest. They shot arrows at a target hung from a pole, raced horses along the sand, and wrestled in a ring of watching villagers. The best archer won a bronze knife from the chief and a jar of wine. Old men compared the youths to the heroes of their youth, and the girls laughed when the boasters slipped in the mud.",
  "choson:drought": "Imagined: the rains failed over the rice and millet fields of Gojoseon (고조선). Day after day the sky stayed white. The wells sank, the river shrank to pools, and the elders led the people up the sacred mountain. There they offered a pig and millet wine to the spirit of the sky and beat drums so loud that the clouds might hear. The children carried water in jars from the last springs, and the families waited, hungry, watching the west.",
  "choson:quake": "Imagined: one night the hills of Gojoseon (고조선) shook. Pit houses caved in, roof beams fell into the hearths, and in the morning a crack ran across the fields. The shaman said the earth spirit was angry and danced with her bronze mirror and bells to calm it. The villagers dug their neighbours out of the collapsed houses and rebuilt them over the same hearths. For a long time afterward, children were told to sleep near the door.",
  "choson:winter": "Imagined: the winter in the north of Gojoseon (고조선) was long and bitter. The rivers froze hard, and snow buried the paths between villages. Families sank their houses half into the ground, kept a fire burning in the hearth day and night, and lived on dried millet, acorns and smoked meat. The hunters went out on the snow after deer. Elders told the story of the bear who endured a hundred days in a dark cave to become a woman.",
  "choson:plague": "Imagined: a sickness came up the river with traders to a Gojoseon (고조선) village, and person after person burned with fever. The shaman danced through the night with bells and a bronze mirror, calling on the spirits to take the sickness away. The sick were laid in a separate house, and their families left food at the door. When it had passed, the dead were buried under stones, and the village gathered by the dolmen to give thanks.",
  "choson:wedding": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells the founding story of Gojoseon (고조선). Hwanung (환웅), son of heaven, came down to Mount Taebaek (태백산). A bear and a tiger asked to become human; he gave them mugwort and garlic and told them to stay out of the sun for a hundred days. The tiger gave up, but the bear became a woman, Ungnyeo (웅녀). She prayed under the sandalwood tree for a child. Hwanung married her, and their son was Dangun (단군), the founder.",

  // ── Mongols: siege … wedding ──
  "mongols:siege": "In 1203 the Kereit (ᠬᠡᠷᠡᠶᠢᠳ / Хэрэйд) ruler Ong Khan (ᠣᠩ ᠬᠠᠨ / Ван хан) turned on Temüjin (ᠲᠡᠮᠦᠵᠢᠨ / Тэмүжин) and smashed his army. Temüjin fled with a handful of followers to Baljuna (ᠪᠠᠯᠵᠤᠨ᠎ᠠ / Балжуна), a muddy lake in the east, hunted and nearly destroyed. There, the story goes, they killed a wild horse for food, and Temüjin raised a cup of the muddy water and swore to share sweet and bitter with those who stood by him. The Baljuna drinkers were honoured all their lives.",
  "mongols:triumph": "Imagined: in autumn the riders came home across the steppe after a long campaign. From far off the camp saw the dust and the horse-tail standard (ᠰᠦᠯᠳᠡ / сүлд). The women came out of the gers (ᠭᠡᠷ / гэр) with bowls of airag (ᠠᠶᠢᠷᠠᠭ / айраг), flicking drops of milk to the sky for luck, and the old men counted the faces. That night a singer played the horse-head fiddle (ᠮᠣᠷᠢᠨ ᠬᠤᠭᠤᠷ / морин хуур) and the long songs went on until the stars faded.",
  "mongols:build": "In the 1250s the Flemish friar William of Rubruck visited the Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) capital Karakorum (ᠬᠠᠷᠠᠬᠣᠷᠣᠮ / Хархорум) at the court of Möngke Khan (ᠮᠥᠩᠬᠡ / Мөнх хаан). He saw a silver tree made by the captive Parisian goldsmith Guillaume Boucher: four snakes coiled around it, each pouring a drink, wine, mare's milk, rice wine and mead, and an angel on top blew a trumpet to signal the servants. Craftsmen from across the world had built the city together.",
  "mongols:voyage": "Imagined: each spring and autumn a Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) family packed up its ger (ᠭᠡᠷ / гэр), loading the felt walls and lattice onto ox carts and camels, and drove the herds to new pastures. Children rode ahead on the sheep's flanks; grandmothers sat on the carts with the iron stove. They stopped at the ovoo (ᠣᠪᠣᠭ᠎ᠠ / овоо) cairns, circled them three times, added a stone. On the last day they saw the river valley green ahead, and the whole family sang.",
  "mongols:festival": "Tsagaan Sar (ᠴᠠᠭᠠᠨ ᠰᠠᠷ᠎ᠠ / Цагаан сар), the White Month, is the Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) new year. Marco Polo wrote that at the White Feast Kublai Khan (ᠬᠤᠪᠢᠯᠠᠢ / Хубилай хаан) was given a hundred thousand white horses, and everyone dressed in white for luck. In every ger families greet their elders by holding them under the arms, lay out a tower of biscuits, a boiled sheep's back, and steam hundreds of buuz (ᠪᠤᠤᠵᠠ / бууз) dumplings, and the young visit every relative in turn.",
  "mongols:games": "At Naadam (ᠨᠠᠭᠠᠳᠣᠮ / Наадам) in midsummer, the three manly games are held: wrestling (ᠪᠥᠬᠡ / бөх), horse racing and archery. Wrestlers in open jackets and pointed boots dance the eagle dance before each bout. In the races, children of five to twelve ride bareback for up to thirty kilometres across the steppe, singing to their horses. The archers, women among them, shoot leather targets on the ground, and the judges sing out each hit with a long call (ᠤᠤᠬᠠᠢ / уухай).",
  "mongols:drought": "Imagined: in a dry summer the grass on the steppe turned brown by midsummer and the wells failed one by one. A Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) family watched their sheep grow thin. The father rode out for days looking for pasture, and the elders made an offering at the ovoo (ᠣᠪᠣᠭ᠎ᠠ / овоо), pouring milk to the sky (ᠲᠩᠷᠢ / Тэнгэр). In the end they moved far to the north, joining other families at a distant spring, sharing water with strangers. Rain came in autumn; half the lambs had died.",
  "mongols:quake": "On 23 July 1905 one of the greatest earthquakes ever recorded struck the Khangai (ᠬᠠᠩᠭᠠᠢ / Хангай) mountains: the Bolnai (Болнай) earthquake, magnitude about eight, following the Tsetserleg (Цэцэрлэг) quake days before. A crack hundreds of kilometres long tore across the steppe. Herders felt the ground roll like waves, horses fell, and gers (ᠭᠡᠷ / гэр) collapsed; because people lived in felt tents rather than houses, few died. The lamas held prayers in the monasteries for weeks afterward.",
  "mongols:winter": "Imagined: the dzud (ᠵᠣᠳ / зуд) came after a dry summer: a winter when ice locked the grass under the snow and the animals could not graze. A Mongol (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ / Монгол) herder dug through the crust with his hands for his sheep, and fed them the felt from his own ger lining. Night after night the cold dropped below minus forty. One by one the animals died. In spring the family counted what was left, burned juniper to purify the camp, and began again.",
  "mongols:plague": "In 1346 the army of Janibeg (ᠵᠠᠨᠢᠪᠡᠭ / Жанибек), khan of the Golden Horde (ᠠᠯᠲᠠᠨ ᠣᠷᠳᠤ / Алтан Орд), was camped outside the port of Caffa in Crimea when plague swept through the camp. The soldiers died by thousands; their bodies lay in heaps. The siege was abandoned. A writer of the time claimed the dead were thrown over the walls, though historians are unsure. The disease went on by ship to Europe, and back across the steppe to the herders' camps.",
  "mongols:wedding": "When Temüjin (ᠲᠡᠮᠦᠵᠢᠨ / Тэмүжин) was nine, his father took him to Dei Sechen (ᠳᠡᠢ ᠰᠡᠴᠡᠨ / Дэй сэцэн), who had dreamed that a white gyrfalcon brought the sun and moon to his hand. He gave his daughter Börte (ᠪᠥᠷᠲᠡ / Бөртэ) to the boy. Years later Temüjin returned to marry her. Her mother sent a black sable coat as a gift for his family. The Secret History (ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ ᠤᠨ ᠨᠢᠭᠤᠴᠠ ᠲᠣᠪᠴᠢᠶᠠᠨ / Монголын нууц товчоо) tells it all: the bride, the dream, and the sable, which Temüjin would give to win an ally.",

  // ── Jurchens: siege … wedding ──
  "jurchens:siege": "In 1233 the last Jin emperor Aizong (金哀宗) fled to Caizhou (蔡州) with what remained of his court. Mongol (蒙古) and Song (宋) armies surrounded the city. Inside, the Jurchen (女真) defenders ate their horses, then saddles and leather, then anything they could find. In January 1234, as the walls were breached, Aizong passed the throne to his general Wanyan Chenglin (完颜承麟) and hanged himself. Chenglin, emperor for less than a day, was killed in the street fighting. The soldiers fought to the end.",
  "jurchens:triumph": "In 1114 the Jurchen (女真) chief Wanyan Aguda (完颜阿骨打) rose against the Liao (辽) with about 2,500 warriors and defeated them at Ningjiang (宁江州). In 1115 he crushed a far larger Liao army at Chuhedian (出河店). The riders came home to the Anchuhu river (按出虎水) with horses, armour and grain, and the clans gathered in celebration. That year Aguda proclaimed a new state, the Jin (金), Golden, after the river whose name meant gold.",
  "jurchens:build": "From 1189 to 1192 the Jin (金) built the Lugou Bridge (卢沟桥) over the Yongding (永定河) river near their capital Zhongdu (中都). Its eleven stone arches carried the road south; its parapets were lined with carved stone lions, each one different, so many that a saying grew: the lions of Lugou cannot be counted. Imagined: Jurchen (女真) overseers and Chinese masons worked side by side, hauling blocks with ropes to a shared chant. Marco Polo later praised it as a bridge with no equal.",
  "jurchens:voyage": "After the Jin (金) conquered northern China, many Jurchen (女真) families were moved south from their forests in Manchuria (满洲) to the plains of Hebei (河北) and Shandong (山东), organised as meng'an mouke (猛安谋克) units of soldiers and farmers. Imagined: a family loaded their carts and set off, the children wrapped in furs. Week after week they rode south until the forests gave way to wheat fields and walled towns. They kept their own speech for a generation, then slowly learned Chinese ways.",
  "jurchens:festival": "Song (宋) writers reported an unusual Jurchen (女真) custom: on the sixteenth night of the first month, after the New Year, theft was allowed (放偷). People could carry off anything from their neighbours, carts, horses, even a girl who had agreed to elope, and nobody was punished. Imagined: young men crept laughing through the snow to steal their neighbours' wine jars and saddles, and the next day families went round bargaining, with wine and cakes, to buy their things back.",
  "jurchens:games": "At the Double Fifth (重五) festival the Jin court held the bai tian (拜天) rite to heaven, then shot at willow branches and played polo (击球). Riders on fast horses carried sticks with crescent heads and chased a small ball across the field toward goals set up at one end. Jurchen (女真) princes were famous for their skill, and drums beat with each goal. Emperor Shizong (金世宗) loved the game so much that his ministers begged him to stop for his own safety.",
  "jurchens:drought": "Imagined: in the last years of the Jin (金), drought and locusts struck the lands around Kaifeng (开封). The wheat withered in the fields, and refugees streamed into the city. Jurchen (女真) soldiers and Chinese farmers alike stood in lines for gruel at the gates of the granary. Prices rose so high that a silk robe bought only a few pounds of grain. Families sold their furniture, then their winter clothes, and waited for an autumn that did not bring rain.",
  "jurchens:quake": "Imagined: in winter the ground shook beneath a Jurchen (女真) village in the hills along the Songhua (松花江). Snow slid off the roofs, the timber houses creaked, and the clay kang stoves cracked. The families ran outside into the cold. The shaman (萨满) beat her drum to calm the earth spirit, and the hunters went from house to house to check on the old people. They rebuilt the stoves first, before the night's frost could take anyone.",
  "jurchens:winter": "Imagined: in the forests of the Changbai mountains (长白山) the winter was long and cruel. Jurchen (女真) families lived in half-buried houses heated by kang (炕), clay platforms with the stove's smoke running through them, and slept on the warm bed together. The men went out on skis and snowshoes after sable and deer, the women sewed furs and kept the fire alive. When the cold broke at last, they took the dogs to the river to hear the ice crack.",
  "jurchens:plague": "In the spring of 1232, after the Mongol (蒙古) siege of the Jin capital Kaifeng (开封), a terrible epidemic broke out inside the walls. The History of Jin (金史) says that in fifty days more than 900,000 coffins were carried out of the city gates, besides the poor who could not afford one. The doctor Li Gao (李杲) was there and wrote that the sickness came from hunger and fear as much as from contagion. The Jin court never recovered.",
  "jurchens:wedding": "Song (宋) accounts describe Jurchen (女真) marriage customs. A young man went to his bride's family with gifts of horses and wine and served in their household for some years before taking her home. Poorer families had another way: their daughters walked along the road singing songs about their own looks, skills and family, so a man who liked her singing could take her home. Imagined: at the wedding feast, the guests drank from horn cups and danced to the beat of drums.",

  // ── Khitans: siege … wedding ──
  "khitans:siege": "In 1122 the Jurchen (女真) armies swept over the Liao (辽) lands, and the last Liao emperor, Tianzuo (天祚帝), fled with a dwindling following into the Jiashan mountains (夹山). For three years he and his Khitan (契丹) riders hid in the valleys there, hunted, cold and hungry. They ate snow when the water froze and wild game when they could find it. Many slipped away; a few stayed loyal. In 1125 Tianzuo rode out to try to reach the Tanguts (党项) and was captured on the way.",
  "khitans:triumph": "Imagined: when the Khitan (契丹) riders returned from a summer campaign, the Liao (辽) camp went out to meet them across the grass. The emperor offered a white horse and a grey ox at the shrine to the ancestors at Mount Muye (木叶山). Captured horses and goods were divided among the clans. That night the riders drank kumis and wine from silver cups, the drums and pipa played, and the warriors danced in a ring while the old men sang of victories past.",
  "khitans:build": "In 1056, under the Liao (辽), the Khitans (契丹) and their Chinese craftsmen raised the wooden pagoda of the Fogong Temple (佛宫寺释迦塔) at Yingxian (应县). Sixty-seven metres tall, it was built without a single iron nail, its beams held by dozens of kinds of interlocking brackets (斗拱). It has stood through earthquakes and wars for nearly a thousand years, the oldest and tallest wooden pagoda in the world. Imagined: the carpenters sang as they raised each level.",
  "khitans:voyage": "In 1124, as the Liao fell, the Khitan prince Yelu Dashi (耶律大石) broke away with about two hundred riders and rode west. At Kedun (可敦城) he gathered tribes and men; then he led them across the deserts and mountains of Central Asia. In 1141 his army won at Qatwan, and he founded the Western Liao, Qara Khitai (西辽). Imagined: after months on the road, the Khitans (契丹) saw the green valley of the Chu river and pitched their tents.",
  "khitans:festival": "The History of Liao (辽史) describes the Khitan New Year. Lumps of sticky rice and white mutton fat were rolled into balls the size of a fist, forty-nine for each tent. Before dawn everyone threw them out of the tent windows and counted them: an even number meant music and a feast; an odd number meant twelve shamans had to circle the tent with bells and arrows, shouting, while salt was cracked in the stove to frighten away ghosts (惊鬼).",
  "khitans:games": "On the third day of the third month, the History of Liao (辽史) says, Khitans (契丹) held the rabbit shooting (射兔). They carved a rabbit out of wood and set it up as a target; riders galloped past and shot at it. The first to hit it won, and the losers had to kneel and offer the winner wine, which he drank from horseback. They also played polo (击鞠) on the grasslands, and Khitan princes were famous for their speed.",
  "khitans:drought": "Imagined: a dry year scorched the steppe of the Liao (辽), and the grass died early. Khitan (契丹) herders drove their flocks from one dried spring to the next. The emperor led the sese rite (瑟瑟仪), shooting arrows at willow branches to call the rain, but the sky stayed clear. Farmers in the southern prefectures lost their millet. The court opened the granaries of the Southern Capital (南京) to feed the hungry, and the herders slaughtered weak animals for meat.",
  "khitans:quake": "In 1057 a great earthquake struck the Liao Southern Capital (南京, today's Beijing). The History of Liao (辽史) records that the city walls and the houses of the people collapsed, and tens of thousands were killed or injured. Survivors camped in the open, and the court sent grain and cloth to the injured. In the Buddhist temples monks chanted day and night. The Khitans (契丹), who lived in tents on the steppe, had seldom feared the ground before.",
  "khitans:winter": "Every winter the Liao (辽) emperor moved his court to the winter camp (冬捺钵) at Guangpingdian (广平淀), a sheltered plain where the snow was thinner. The emperor's tent was surrounded by a wall of carts and felt. Khitan (契丹) nobles hunted and held council, while the herders struggled to keep their animals alive through the blizzards. Imagined: a family whose herds were dying in the snow cut ice holes to water their horses and prayed to the spirit of the sun.",
  "khitans:plague": "Imagined: a fever spread through the Khitan (契丹) camps one summer, carried by merchants from the south. Families who lived together in tents fell ill together. The shamans beat their drums and burned juniper, the sick were moved to separate tents, and the camps moved to new pasture to leave the sickness behind. The emperor ordered medicines sent from the Southern Capital (南京). Those who died were laid out on the mountain trees, as Khitan custom required.",
  "khitans:wedding": "Liao (辽) emperors married only women of the Xiao (萧) clan, so the two families were bound in every generation. In a Liao wedding, accounts say, the bride's family laid a saddle on the ground at the tent door, and the bride stepped over it as she entered: a saddle (鞍) sounds like peace (安). The custom of stepping over a saddle spread from the Khitans (契丹) into Chinese weddings. Imagined: the drums sounded, and the guests drank kumis until dawn.",
  // ── 楽 showcase: histories of each people's music ──
  "chinese:showcase": "Ming (明) and Qing (清) music inherited the ritual bells, the scholar's guqin (古琴), the Tang (唐) pipa (琵琶) and the bowed huqin (胡琴) of earlier ages, but its great growth was in opera (戏曲). Kunqu (昆曲) ruled the Ming stage; under the Qing, local styles such as bangzi (梆子) clapper opera and the pihuang (皮黄) tunes merged in Beijing into Peking opera (京剧), led by the jinghu (京胡) fiddle. Manchu (满洲) court banquets set Mongol and other peoples' music beside ritual music (雅乐). In towns and villages, suona (唢呐) bands played at weddings and funerals, and silk-and-bamboo (丝竹) ensembles flourished. The erhu (二胡), pipa, dizi (笛子), sheng (笙), guzheng (古筝) and ruan (阮) heard in this piece are all still played today.",
  "shang:showcase": "Shang (商) music is known only from what was buried at Anyang (安阳) and from oracle-bone inscriptions (甲骨文), which name dances, drums and offerings to ancestors. Excavations have found clay xun (埙) ocarinas with several finger holes, stone chimes (磬), and sets of bronze nao (铙) bells held mouth-up and struck. Drums of alligator skin (鼍鼓) were already known in earlier Neolithic graves. The Zhou (周) who followed built on Shang bells and chimes to create their great ritual orchestras. No Shang melody survives, so this piece is a reconstruction: xun leads, bianqing (编磬) chimes answer, with nao bells and the tuogu drum, and a few Zhou-era stand-ins such as the se (瑟) zither.",
  "shu:showcase": "The Sichuan basin had its own Bronze Age culture at Sanxingdui (三星堆), but its music is mostly guessed from bronzes. The Ba (巴) people struck the chunyu (錞于), a bronze bell-drum often crowned with a tiger, and their war dance, the Ba-Yu dance (巴渝舞), so impressed the Han (汉) court that it became a court dance. Han tomb figures from Sichuan show drumming storytellers and players of zithers and pipes. The region is also remembered for the qin: Sima Xiangru (司马相如) of Chengdu won Zhuo Wenjun (卓文君) with a qin song, and the Shu school (蜀派) of guqin (古琴) playing survives today. In this piece the xiao (箫) flute leads, answered by guqin, with sheng (笙), bianzhong (编钟) bells and the chunyu.",
  "wei:showcase": "The Yellow River heartland is where early Chinese ritual music took shape, with Zhou (周) bells and chimes and the songs gathered in the Book of Songs (诗经). Under the Han (汉), armies and processions marched to guchui (鼓吹) bands of drums, horns, panpipes (排箫) and transverse flutes (横吹), and the words of their nao songs (铙歌) were written down. In the Wei (魏) and Jin (晋), Ruan Xian (阮咸), one of the Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove (竹林七贤), was famed for a round-bodied lute later named the ruan (阮) after him, and the konghou (箜篌) harp arrived from the west. Wind-and-drum bands (吹打) still play at village festivals. This piece uses hengchui, ruan, paixiao and the jiangu (建鼓) drum.",
  "wu:showcase": "The lower Yangtze had its own bronze music: the Wu (吴) and Yue (越) cast goudiao (句鑃), bells held mouth-up, and Yue tombs hold bells and chimes made of proto-porcelain. Soft Wu songs (吴歌) and southern love songs (吴声歌曲) were collected in the Southern Dynasties (南朝). In the Ming (明) the singer Wei Liangfu (魏良辅) refined the Kunshan tune (昆山腔) into Kunqu (昆曲), sung to the qudi (曲笛) flute. Jiangnan pipa (琵琶) players formed their own schools, Suzhou pingtan (评弹) storytellers sing to pipa and sanxian, and silk-and-bamboo (江南丝竹) ensembles still meet in teahouses of Shanghai and Suzhou. In this piece the pipa leads, answered by qudi, with guzheng (古筝) and sheng (笙).",
  "zhu-xis-legacy:showcase": "Song (宋) scholars saw music as part of the moral order of ritual and music (礼乐). Emperor Huizong (徽宗) had new bronze bells cast for court ritual music (雅乐), modelled on an ancient bell. Zhu Xi (朱熹) wrote on qin tuning, and his Yili jingzhuan tongjie (仪礼经传通解) preserves twelve Book of Songs melodies (风雅十二诗谱), said to come down from the Tang (唐). In their studies the literati played the guqin (古琴), and qin handbooks in abbreviated tablature (减字谱) kept the repertoire alive. Confucian temple music across East Asia grew from this revival, and qin players still perform Song-era pieces such as Xiaoxiang shuiyun (潇湘水云). This piece pairs guqin and se (瑟) with bianzhong (编钟) and bianqing (编磬).",
  "yamato:showcase": "Music in the Kofun (古墳) age is known only from objects. Clay haniwa (埴輪) figures show men seated with zithers across their laps, and others with drums; before them the Yayoi (弥生) people cast bronze bells, dōtaku (銅鐸). The six-string wagon (和琴) belongs to this old native layer and is still played in Shinto rites (御神楽). From the 5th to the 8th centuries musicians from the Korean kingdoms and Tang (唐) China brought new instruments, and at Nara (奈良) and Heian (平安) these became gagaku (雅楽), with hichiriki (篳篥), shō (笙) and ryūteki (龍笛). The Shōsōin (正倉院) still holds Nara-era instruments, and gagaku is performed today. Kofun melodies are lost; this piece is a reconstruction in early gagaku colours.",
  "sengoku-daimyo:showcase": "Warrior music in Japan begins with the blind biwa (琵琶) priests who sang the Tale of the Heike (平家物語) to warriors and courtiers. The samurai (武士) patronised Noh (能), whose chant (謡) and nōkan (能管) flute became part of a warrior's education. On campaign, armies signalled with the horagai (法螺貝) conch, drums and gongs. In southern Kyushu the Satsuma biwa (薩摩琵琶) grew as a way of teaching young warriors through martial ballads, and in the same century the shamisen (三味線) arrived from Ryūkyū (琉球). Satsuma biwa, Noh, and conch blowing by mountain ascetics (山伏) are still practised today. In this piece the Satsuma biwa leads, answered by nōkan, with shinobue (篠笛) and jindaiko (陣太鼓) war drums.",
  "choson:showcase": "Almost nothing is known of music in Gojoseon (고조선). Bronze Age graves on the peninsula hold bronze rattles and bells, such as the eight-bell paljuryeong (팔주령), probably shaken by shamans. The oldest song linked to the region is the Gonghuin (공후인, 箜篌引), told in Chinese sources: a ferryman saw a white-haired man drown crossing a river while his wife sang a lament to the konghou harp, and the ferryman's wife Yeo-ok (여옥) set the song to her own gonghu (공후). Later Goguryeo (고구려) tomb murals show zithers, long horns, drums and dancers, and the geomungo (거문고) is said to have been made there. Gojoseon melodies are lost; this piece is a reconstruction with geomungo, gonghu, buk (북) and shaman rattles.",
  "mongols:showcase": "The steppe has been musical for millennia, but early records are few: Chinese sources describe the horn calls and drums of nomad armies, and William of Rubruck, visiting the Mongol (Монгол) court in the 13th century, saw musicians strike up whenever the khan drank. Long song, urtiin duu (уртын дуу), with its slow, wide melodies, and overtone singing, khöömii (хөөмий), grew among herders as songs for horses, landscapes and gatherings. The horse-head fiddle, morin khuur (морин хуур), is told of in legends of a beloved horse whose hair and bones became the first fiddle. Buddhist monasteries added horns and cymbals. Today khöömii, long song and morin khuur are heard across the grasslands and on concert stages, with the tovshuur (товшуур) lute and yatga (ятга) zither.",
  "jurchens:showcase": "The Jurchen (女真) peoples of the Manchurian forests and rivers had a music of shamans and hunters. Shamans (萨满) danced with a frame drum and iron waist bells (腰铃) that clashed as they turned, and the jaw harp, kouxian (口弦), was played among Tungusic peoples. When the Jin (金) dynasty ruled the north in the 12th century, its court took over Song (宋) ritual instruments and banquet music, and Jin-era farces (院本) and medley ballads (诸宫调) flourished; tomb figures of the time show actors and musicians on stage. Later the Manchu (满洲) court kept shaman rites (萨满祭) alongside its other music, and shaman drumming and jaw harps survive in the region today. In this piece the huqin (胡琴) leads, with sanxian (三弦) and suona (唢呐).",
  "khitans:showcase": "The Khitans (契丹) were mobile herders whose emperors moved between seasonal camps (捺钵). After founding the Liao (辽) in the 10th century, the court kept several kinds of music: ritual music (雅乐) and grand banquet music (大乐) inherited from the Tang (唐) and Later Jin (后晋), beside the Khitans' own national music (国乐) and drum-and-wind bands (鼓吹). Liao tomb murals at Xuanhua (宣化) show bands with pipa (琵琶), sheng, flutes, clappers and drums. The bowed xiqin (奚琴), named after the neighbouring Xi (奚) people, became an ancestor of later fiddles such as the Korean haegeum. Khitan songs themselves are lost, so this piece is a reconstruction using xiqin, pipa, the hujia (胡笳) pipe and fangxiang (方响) metal plates.",
  // ── Ming–Qing: stories of the Ming and Qing centuries ──
  "chinese:gather": "In 1552 the physician Li Shizhen (李时珍) of Qizhou (蕲州) began the work of his life, the Compendium of Materia Medica (本草纲目). For twenty-seven years he walked the hills of Hubei, Jiangxi and Jiangsu with a basket on his back, asking herb gatherers, woodcutters, hunters and fishermen what they knew, digging roots and tasting leaves himself. Where the old herbals were wrong he corrected them, one plant at a time. The finished book, printed in 1593 just after his death, described almost nineteen hundred remedies and was soon read in Japan and later in Europe.",
  "chinese:flood": "Four times between 1565 and 1592 the Ming (明) court sent Pan Jixun (潘季驯) to tame the Yellow River (黄河), which kept silting up, breaking its banks and choking the Grand Canal (大运河) that carried the grain north. His answer was to bind the water and let it scour the sand (束水攻沙): narrow, strong dikes so the river ran fast and carried its own silt to the sea. Tens of thousands of labourers hauled earth, stones and bundles of sorghum stalks along the banks, singing to keep the pace. For years afterwards the river held, and the grain boats sailed north again.",
  "chinese:fire": "On the morning of 30 May 1626 the Wanggongchang (王恭厂) gunpowder works in the west of Beijing (北京) blew up. A roar like thunder rolled over the city, a dark cloud rose into the sky, and streets of houses around the works were flattened. The young Tianqi Emperor (天启帝), at breakfast in the palace, ran for safety while roof tiles fell around him. Fires spread through the lanes, and for days people searched the rubble for their families. No one ever learned what set it off, and Beijing told strange tales of that morning for generations.",
  "chinese:siege": "In the first month of 1626 Nurhaci (努尔哈赤), founder of the Later Jin (后金), led a great army across the frozen Liao (辽) river against the small walled town of Ningyuan (宁远). The Ming (明) commander Yuan Chonghuan (袁崇焕) brought the townspeople inside, burned the houses outside the walls so the attackers had no shelter, and mounted heavy cannon cast on a Portuguese model, the red-barbarian guns (红夷大炮), on the ramparts. For days the attackers dug at the frozen walls under fire. Then they withdrew. For the first time in his long career, Nurhaci had been turned back.",
  "chinese:triumph": "In 1697 the Kangxi Emperor (康熙帝) rode out a third time against Galdan (噶尔丹), the Dzungar (准噶尔) khan he had chased across the deserts for years. All through the campaign he wrote home to the palace about the food, the horses, the sand and the cold. In the summer news came that Galdan was dead, and his letters turned to joy. When he came back to Beijing (北京), princes and officials rode out to meet him, the banner drums and horns sounded the victory songs (铙歌), and the court gave thanks to Heaven and the ancestors.",
  "chinese:festival": "On the night of the Mid-Autumn Festival (中秋), wrote Zhang Dai (张岱) in the late Ming (明), all of Suzhou (苏州) climbed Tiger Hill (虎丘): gentry and actors, maids and courtesans, boatmen and shop boys, spreading their mats over the great flat rock. At first a thousand voices sang together with gongs and drums. As the night deepened the drums fell silent and only flutes and strings played; then only the best singers, one by one, in the slow Kunqu (昆曲) style. Near midnight a single man stood up and sang alone, without any instrument, and the whole hill held its breath.",
  "chinese:games": "The Ming (明) Xuande Emperor (宣德帝), who reigned from 1425 to 1435, loved cricket fighting (斗蟋蟀) so much that people called him the cricket emperor. Officials in Suzhou (苏州) were ordered to send the fiercest fighters to the palace, and a champion insect could cost as much as a horse. Around 1680 Pu Songling (蒲松龄) told a sad tale of it, Cricket (促织): a poor man's son kills the family's prize cricket by accident, and his spirit becomes a tiny, fearless cricket that beats every rival. Beijing men still carried their crickets in gourds inside their coats all winter.",
  "chinese:winter": "In Beijing (北京) the winter was counted in nines (数九): nine spans of nine days from the solstice until the cold broke. To pass them, Qing (清) households hung a chart for dispersing the cold (九九消寒图). The Daoguang Emperor (道光帝) is said to have written one with nine characters, 亭前垂柳珍重待春風, each of nine strokes, drawn in outline. Every morning one stroke was filled in with ink, and when the eighty-first was done it was spring. Children chanted the nines song as they went: in the third and fourth nines, you can walk on the ice.",
  // ── Edo: stories of the Edo age ──
  "japanese:battle": "In the summer of 1615, at Osaka (大坂), the last great battle before two and a half centuries of peace was fought. Sanada Nobushige (真田信繁), remembered as Yukimura (幸村), led his red-armoured horsemen (赤備え) straight at the headquarters of Tokugawa Ieyasu (徳川家康) near Tennōji (天王寺). Three times they broke through; Ieyasu's standard fell, and he is said to have thought of taking his own life. Then the charge was spent. Sanada, exhausted, sat down to rest at the Yasui shrine (安居神社) and was killed there. Even his enemies called him the finest warrior in Japan (日本一の兵).",
  "japanese:voyage": "In 1782 the cargo ship Shinshō-maru (神昌丸) sailed from Shiroko (白子) in Ise (伊勢) with rice for Edo (江戸) and was caught by a storm. For eight months it drifted, until the captain, Daikokuya Kōdayū (大黒屋光太夫), and his crew washed up on Amchitka in the Aleutian Islands. They crossed Kamchatka and Siberia, and in 1791 Kōdayū knelt before Catherine the Great in Saint Petersburg to ask to go home. She agreed. In 1792 a Russian ship brought him to Nemuro (根室). Of the seventeen men who had sailed, only three saw Japan again.",
  "japanese:rain": "In 1857 Utagawa Hiroshige (歌川広重) published Sudden Shower over the Ōhashi Bridge and Atake (大はしあたけの夕立), one of his One Hundred Famous Views of Edo (名所江戸百景). Grey lines of rain slant across the whole sheet; a few people hurry over the wooden bridge under straw capes and umbrellas, and the far bank fades into the downpour. Every Edo (江戸) townsman knew that kind of summer evening shower. Thirty years later in Paris, Vincent van Gogh copied the print in oil, rain and all.",
  "japanese:drought": "As Edo (江戸) grew into one of the largest cities in the world, it ran short of drinking water, and in dry summers the wells went brackish. In 1653 the shogunate hired two brothers, Shōemon (庄右衛門) and Seiemon (清右衛門), to bring water from the Tama River (多摩川). In about eight months their workers dug a channel more than forty kilometres long from Hamura (羽村), dropping only a few metres over its whole length. Legend says the brothers sold their own houses when the money ran out. The Tamagawa Aqueduct (玉川上水) watered the city for three centuries, and they were given the name Tamagawa.",
  "japanese:thunder": "Early in the 1600s the Kyoto (京都) painter Tawaraya Sōtatsu (俵屋宗達) painted a pair of gold screens with the Wind God and the Thunder God (風神雷神図屏風). On the left, the green-skinned thunder god Raijin (雷神) leaps across the gold on a dark cloud, beating the ring of drums that circles his back; on the right the wind god grins with his bag of storms. A century later Ogata Kōrin (尾形光琳) copied the screens, and later still Sakai Hōitsu (酒井抱一) copied Kōrin. Each painter answered the drummer in the clouds in his own way.",
  "japanese:wedding": "An Edo (江戸) wedding was held at the groom's house, often in the evening. The bride arrived in white, her face partly hidden, and the couple sealed the marriage with three-three-nine (三三九度): three sips from each of three stacked lacquer cups. Then a guest of the family stood and chanted Takasago (高砂), the Noh (能) song of an old couple who are the spirits of two pine trees: Takasago ya, kono urafune ni ho wo agete, raising a sail on this little boat. The verses wished the pair a long life together, growing old as the pines.",
  "japanese:showcase": "The Edo (江戸) period was the great age of Japanese town music. The shamisen (三味線), made from the Ryūkyū sanshin in the 16th century, became the voice of kabuki (歌舞伎), and from 1684 Takemoto Gidayū (竹本義太夫) chanted the puppet plays of Chikamatsu Monzaemon (近松門左衛門) to the heavy futozao. Yatsuhashi Kengyō (八橋検校), a blind master, shaped solo koto (箏) music such as Rokudan (六段), and blind guilds played koto, shamisen and kokyū (胡弓) together. Komusō (虚無僧) monks of the Fuke (普化) sect wandered in basket hats, playing the shakuhachi (尺八) as meditation. Festival bands of shinobue (篠笛) and taiko (太鼓) played the Kanda (神田) and Sannō (山王) festivals in the streets.",
  // ── Joseon: stories of the Joseon age ──
  "koreans:triumph": "When a young Joseon (조선) scholar passed the state examination (과거), the king's men set a paper flower, the eohwa (어사화), in his hat. Then came the three days of parading (삼일유가): the new graduate rode through the streets of Hanyang (한양) on horseback, preceded by musicians with pipes and drums and by gwangdae (광대) clowns and acrobats who tumbled and sang, while neighbours crowded to look. He called on his teachers and elders, and at last went home to a village feast. From the songs of such clowns, people say, pansori (판소리) grew.",
  "koreans:quake": "The Annals of Joseon (조선왕조실록) record that in the summer of 1518, in the reign of King Jungjong (중종), a great earthquake struck Hanyang (한양). There was a sound like thunder, houses shook as if they would fall, and the city wall cracked and collapsed in places. People ran from their houses and spent the night in the open, afraid to go back under their roofs, and the shocks went on for days. The king and his officials asked anxiously what wrong in the kingdom had moved the earth, and the court cut back its feasting.",
  "koreans:flood": "Every summer the Cheonggyecheon (청계천) stream running through Hanyang (한양) filled with silt and flooded the houses of the poor along its banks. In 1760 King Yeongjo (영조) ordered it dredged (경진준천). In fifty-seven days some two hundred thousand people dug out the bed and lined the banks with stone; some were paid, some came as volunteers. The old king went out to the Ogansumun (오간수문) water gate to watch the work and talk with the labourers. A new office kept the stream clear afterwards, and paintings of the work were made for the officials who had led it.",
  "koreans:fire": "In the second month of 1426, while King Sejong (세종) was away from the capital, a fire broke out in Hanyang (한양) and a strong wind drove it through the crowded streets near the markets. More than two thousand houses burned, and people died in the smoke. Sejong hurried back and set up the Geumhwa Dogam (금화도감), an office to prevent fires: firebreaks were cut between houses, wells and water jars were placed along the streets, and men were organised to fight fires. It is remembered as the first fire brigade in Korea.",
  "koreans:showcase": "Joseon (조선) music had two lives. At court, King Sejong (세종) and the scholar Bak Yeon (박연) reformed the ritual music (아악), and Sejong himself is credited with Yeomillak (여민락) and the music for the royal shrine. The Akhak gwebeom (악학궤범, 1493) drew every instrument. In the later centuries music moved into the countryside and the market towns. Pansori (판소리) singers told long tales to a single buk (북) drum, travelling troupes such as the namsadang (남사당) danced and juggled, and farmers' pungmul (풍물) bands of kkwaenggwari (꽹과리), jing (징), janggu (장구) and taepyeongso (태평소) played at work and festivals. In the 1890s Kim Chang-jo (김창조) shaped sanjo (산조) for the gayageum (가야금).",
  // ── Han, Zhou–Han: stories of the Zhou and Han ages ──
  "chinese-early:showcase": "In 1978 archaeologists opened the tomb of Marquis Yi of Zeng (曾侯乙), buried around 433 BC, and found a whole orchestra: sixty-five bronze bells (编钟) on a lacquered rack, each sounding two notes, stone chimes (编磬), se (瑟) zithers, sheng (笙) mouth organs, panpipes (排箫), chi (篪) flutes and drums. Zhou (周) rulers believed that ritual and music (礼乐) kept the world in order; Confucius (孔子), hearing the ancient Shao (韶) music, forgot the taste of meat for three months. The songs of the Book of Songs (诗经) were sung to such instruments. Under Emperor Wu (汉武帝) the Han court's Music Bureau (乐府), led by Li Yannian (李延年), collected folk songs from every region and set new hymns.",
  "chinese-early:hunt": "Around 135 BC the poet Sima Xiangru (司马相如) wrote the Rhapsody on the Shanglin Park (上林赋) for Emperor Wu (汉武帝), describing the imperial hunting park west of Chang'an (长安). Chariots thunder out at dawn, banners fill the valleys, beaters drive tigers, leopards and deer from the woods, and archers shoot birds from the sky. Then the hunt ends with a feast: bells are struck, drums beaten, singers from every region perform, and dancers sway. At the very end the emperor sighs that all this is too much, and opens the park to the farmers.",
  "chinese-early:battle": "In 204 BC the Han (汉) general Han Xin (韩信) marched through the narrow Jingxing pass (井陉) against a much larger army of Zhao (赵). He did the unthinkable: he drew up ten thousand men with their backs to a river, where they could not run. The Zhao troops poured out of their camp to finish them; the trapped Han soldiers fought like men with nowhere to go. Meanwhile two thousand riders slipped into the empty Zhao camp and raised red Han flags on its walls. Seeing them, the Zhao army broke. Fighting with your back to the water (背水一战) became a proverb.",
  "chinese-early:siege": "In the 5th century BC, the story goes, the engineer Gongshu Ban (公输班) built new scaling ladders for the state of Chu (楚) to attack the little state of Song (宋). The philosopher Mozi (墨子), who preached universal love and condemned aggressive war, walked ten days and nights to reach the Chu king. Before him, Mozi untied his belt to make a city wall and used wooden slips for machines. Nine times Gongshu Ban attacked with his models; nine times Mozi held. His students, he added, were already on the walls of Song. The king called off the war.",
  "chinese-early:triumph": "In 101 BC, after a long and costly campaign, a Han (汉) army came back from Dayuan (大宛) in Ferghana with the famous blood-sweating horses (汗血马) that Emperor Wu (汉武帝) had longed for. Overjoyed, the emperor had a hymn made, the Song of the Heavenly Horses (天马歌), for the Music Bureau (乐府) to perform at the court's great sacrifices: the heavenly horses have come from the far west, crossing ten thousand li of flowing sand. Han musicians sang it with bells and drums, and bronze horses in full gallop were cast for the tombs of the age.",
  "chinese-early:gather": "One of the songs of the Book of Songs (诗经), Plantain (芣苢), is nothing but women gathering. Pick, pick the plantain, here we pick it; pick, pick the plantain, here we have it. Verse by verse they pluck the seeds, strip the stems, fill their skirts and tuck up their aprons to carry the harvest home. Zhou (周) singers repeated the lines as the women repeated their work in the fields, and two thousand years later scholars still argued over what the plant cured. The song itself needed no explanation: it is the sound of work shared in the sun.",
  "chinese-early:build": "Around 256 BC Li Bing (李冰), governor of Shu (蜀) for the state of Qin (秦), set out to stop the Min river (岷江) flooding the Chengdu (成都) plain. At Dujiangyan (都江堰) his workers built a fish-mouth levee in mid-river to split the water, and cut a channel through solid rock with fire and cold water to lead half of it into the fields. They had no iron explosives, only fire, water, picks and baskets of stones in bamboo cages. The system still waters the plain today, and the people still honour Li Bing and his son at a temple on the river.",
  "chinese-early:voyage": "In 138 BC Zhang Qian (张骞) set out west from Chang'an (长安) with about a hundred men and a guide named Ganfu (甘父), sent by Emperor Wu (汉武帝) to find the distant Yuezhi (月氏). Within days the Xiongnu (匈奴) captured him. He lived among them for ten years, married and had a son, but kept the emperor's staff of office. At last he escaped, crossed the Pamirs and reached the Yuezhi and Bactria. Thirteen years after leaving, he came home with only Ganfu, but full of news of horses, grapes and cities no Han eye had ever seen.",
  "chinese-early:festival": "In the spring, says a song of the state of Zheng (郑) in the Book of Songs (诗经), the Zhen (溱) and Wei (洧) rivers ran high with melted snow. Young men and women went down to the banks holding orchids, as the custom was, to wash away the winter's ill luck. A girl says: shall we go and look? A boy says: I've been already. Well, let's go again. Across the river the land is wide and pleasant. They laugh together and give each other peonies. Later Han (汉) writers called this the Shangsi (上巳) festival of the third month.",
  "chinese-early:games": "Cuju (蹴鞠), kicking a ball stuffed with feathers or hair, was a soldiers' game in the Han (汉). The historian Sima Qian (司马迁) wrote of the young general Huo Qubing (霍去病) that on campaign beyond the Great Wall, when his men were short of food and could hardly stand, he had a field cleared and played ball. Sima Qian did not approve. But Han training manuals listed cuju among the arts of war, tomb carvings show players leaping with the ball, and in the palace and the market alike people gathered to watch a good kicker.",
  "chinese-early:ritual": "In 110 BC Emperor Wu (汉武帝) climbed Mount Tai (泰山) to perform the feng and shan (封禅) sacrifices to Heaven and Earth, rites so ancient that no one remembered exactly how they were done. The court historian Sima Tan (司马谈) fell ill on the way and could not go on. Dying, he told his son Sima Qian (司马迁): the Son of Heaven performs the great rite and I am not allowed to be there; it is fate. He asked his son to finish the history he had begun. Sima Qian promised, and wrote the Records of the Grand Historian (史记).",
  "chinese-early:rain": "When the rain did not come, wrote the Han (汉) scholar Dong Zhongshu (董仲舒) around 130 BC, the officials of every district should pray for it. His instructions in the Luxuriant Dew (春秋繁露) are careful: shut the south gates of the town and open the north ones, forbid fire, build a great dragon of clay and set smaller dragons beside it, and have children dance, eight together, before the altar. Women should be cheerful and men keep quiet. In villages across the Han world people made their clay dragons and waited for the clouds.",
  "chinese-early:drought": "The Book of Songs (诗经) keeps the voice of a Zhou (周) king in a terrible drought. In the poem The Milky Way (云汉), King Xuan (周宣王) looks up at the night sky, bright and cloudless. The drought is too great, he says; the heat burns like fire. I have offered to every spirit and spared no victim; I have given up all the jades and silks, and still no one hears me. Of all the people of Zhou not one will be left. The poem does not end with rain, only with a king who keeps praying for his people.",
  "chinese-early:flood": "In 132 BC the Yellow River (黄河) broke through its banks at Huzi (瓠子) and flooded sixteen commanderies. For more than twenty years it ran wild. In 109 BC Emperor Wu (汉武帝) came himself to the breach. He ordered every official below the generals to carry bundles of brushwood with their own hands, and bamboo from the imperial park was cut to make the dam. Watching the water, he composed the Songs of Huzi (瓠子歌), asking the river god to return to the old course. The gap was closed, and a Palace of Proclaiming the Dike (宣房宫) was built over it.",
  "chinese-early:wind": "In 195 BC Liu Bang (刘邦), the first Han (汉) emperor, was on his way back from a war and stopped at his hometown of Pei (沛). He called together the old friends and elders of the town for a feast and gathered a hundred and twenty boys to sing. When the wine had gone round, he struck the zhu (筑) zither himself and sang: a great wind rises, the clouds fly high; my might covers the land, and I come home; where shall I find brave men to guard the four corners? Then he got up and danced, and tears ran down his face. It is the Song of the Great Wind (大风歌).",
  "chinese-early:thunder": "Most people of the Han (汉) believed that thunder was Heaven punishing the wicked: a man struck by lightning must have hidden some wrong, perhaps feeding people unclean food. Around 80 AD the scholar Wang Chong (王充) disagreed in his book Lunheng (论衡). Thunder, he wrote, is fire: people struck by lightning are burned, the air smells of burning, and stones are scorched. It comes from yin and yang clashing in summer, not from an angry god with drums. Few listened then, but his careful doubting made him one of the first rational voices in China.",
  "chinese-early:quake": "In 132 AD the court astronomer Zhang Heng (张衡) showed the Han (汉) court an instrument of bronze, shaped like a wine jar, with eight dragons around it, each holding a ball in its mouth above an open-mouthed toad. One day in 138 a ball dropped from the western dragon, though no one in the capital Luoyang (洛阳) had felt a thing. Courtiers laughed at the machine. Days later a rider came in from Longxi (陇西), far to the west, reporting a great earthquake. From then on, the history says, the court recorded the direction of every quake.",
  "chinese-early:winter": "In 100 BC the Han (汉) envoy Su Wu (苏武) was detained by the Xiongnu (匈奴), and when he would not change sides they threw him into an empty cellar without food. Snow fell, and he lay chewing snow and the wool of his felt cloak, and lived. They sent him to the frozen shores of Lake Baikal (北海) to herd rams, saying he could go home when the rams gave milk. He dug up wild rats' stores of grain to eat and held his envoy's staff until its tassels fell off. Nineteen years later he came home, his hair white.",
  "chinese-early:plague": "At the end of the Han (汉) epidemics swept the country again and again. The physician Zhang Zhongjing (张仲景) wrote that within ten years two thirds of his clan of more than two hundred people had died, most of cold-damage fevers. Grieving, he studied the old books and his own patients and wrote the Treatise on Cold Damage (伤寒杂病论), which doctors still read. A later legend says that one winter he cooked mutton and herbs wrapped in dough shaped like ears to heal the frostbitten ears of the poor, and that is how dumplings (饺子) began.",
  "chinese-early:birth": "The Book of Rites (礼记) tells how a Zhou (周) household greeted a birth. For a boy, a bow was hung at the left of the door; for a girl, a cloth handkerchief at the right. On the third day the boy was carried out and an archer shot six arrows to heaven, earth and the four directions. A song in the Book of Songs (诗经) goes further: a son is laid on a bed and given a jade sceptre to play with; a daughter is laid on the floor and given a clay spindle whorl. The words were unequal; the joy at a new child was not.",
  "chinese-early:wedding": "Around 140 BC the poet Sima Xiangru (司马相如), poor and unknown, was a guest at a feast given by the rich ironmaster Zhuo Wangsun (卓王孙) of Linqiong (临邛). Zhuo's daughter Zhuo Wenjun (卓文君), lately widowed, loved music, and listened from behind a screen as Sima played the qin (琴) and sang The Phoenix Seeks His Mate (凤求凰). That night she ran away with him to Chengdu (成都), where his house had nothing but four bare walls. Later they came back and opened a wine shop; she served at the counter, he washed the cups, and her father finally gave in.",
  "chinese-early:death": "When the wife of the philosopher Zhuangzi (庄子) died, his friend Huizi (惠子) came to mourn and found him sitting on the ground with his legs sprawled, drumming on a basin and singing. Huizi was shocked: she lived with you, raised your children and grew old with you. Isn't this too much? Zhuangzi answered that at first he had grieved like anyone. But before her life there was no form, then came breath, then a body; now she has changed again, as spring turns to autumn. She is sleeping in the great room of heaven and earth. Why should I wail?",
  // ── Han, Sui–Tang–Song: stories of the Sui, Tang and Song ages ──
  "chinese-middle:showcase": "Sui (隋) and Tang (唐) court banquets were played by the ten ensembles (十部伎), named for their homelands: Kucha (龟兹), Kashgar, Bukhara, India, Korea and more, beside the Chinese pieces. Central Asian musicians brought the crook-necked pipa (琵琶), the konghou (箜篌) harp, the bili (筚篥) and the jiegu (羯鼓) drum, which Emperor Xuanzong (唐玄宗) loved to play himself. He trained hundreds of musicians in the Pear Garden (梨园), and the Rainbow Skirt and Feathered Dress (霓裳羽衣曲) became the most famous piece of the age. Pipa tablatures from Dunhuang (敦煌) still survive. In Song (宋) cities, singers in the entertainment quarters sang ci (词) lyrics, and the poet Jiang Kui (姜夔) wrote down his own tunes in 1202.",
  "chinese-middle:hunt": "When Prince Zhanghuai (章怀太子), a son of the Tang (唐) empress Wu Zetian (武则天), was reburied in 706, painters covered the walls of his tomb passage near Chang'an (长安) with a great hunting procession (狩猎出行图). More than forty riders gallop under the trees, banners flying; falconers carry hawks on their wrists, a hound runs beside a horse, and behind one rider a spotted hunting cat sits on the saddle, brought from Central Asia. Camels follow with the supplies. The painters showed what Tang nobles loved: fast horses, open country and a day of sport together.",
  "chinese-middle:battle": "In the summer of 1140 the Song (宋) general Yue Fei (岳飞) met the Jin (金) cavalry at Yancheng (郾城). The Jin sent in their armoured horsemen, chained together in threes, it was said, so that none could turn back. Yue Fei sent foot soldiers with long axes and hooked blades to crawl in under them and cut the horses' legs; when one horse fell, its partners fell with it. The battle raged till dark and the Jin pulled back. Their soldiers afterwards said: it is easier to shake a mountain than to shake Yue Fei's army (撼山易，撼岳家军难).",
  "chinese-middle:gather": "Around 760 Lu Yu (陆羽), an orphan raised by monks, wrote the Classic of Tea (茶经). Tea, he said, should be picked in the second to fourth months, on a clear morning with the dew still on the leaves, never on a rainy or cloudy day. The best grows wild among rocks; the next in gravelly soil. He described how pickers carried bamboo baskets up the hills, and how the leaves were steamed, pounded, pressed into cakes, dried and strung on cords. Tang (唐) tea houses later set up a figure of Lu Yu as the patron of tea.",
  "chinese-middle:build": "Around 600, under the Sui (隋), a craftsman named Li Chun (李春) built a stone bridge over the Xiao (洨) river at Zhaozhou (赵州). Instead of a high half-circle, he made one long, shallow arch of thirty-seven metres, and opened four small arches in its shoulders so floodwater could pass through and the bridge would weigh less. It has carried people, carts and donkeys for fourteen centuries, through floods and earthquakes. Folk songs later told that the immortal Lu Ban (鲁班) built it in a single night, and that the marks of a donkey's hooves on it are from a god crossing over.",
  "chinese-middle:voyage": "In 742 Japanese monks came to the Tang (唐) master Jianzhen (鉴真) in Yangzhou (扬州) and begged him to send someone to teach in Japan. No one volunteered, so he went himself. Five times he tried to cross: officials stopped him, ships were wrecked, and once a storm blew them all the way to Hainan (海南). On the way back his Japanese disciple died, and Jianzhen lost his sight. In 753, at the sixth attempt and aged sixty-six, he reached Japan, where he founded the Tōshōdai-ji (唐招提寺) temple in Nara. A statue of him, eyes closed, is still kept there.",
  "chinese-middle:ritual": "In the spring of 873 the Tang (唐) emperor Yizong (懿宗) had a finger bone of the Buddha brought from the Famen Temple (法门寺) to Chang'an (长安). For miles the road was lined with silk canopies and incense burners; monks chanted, bands played, and people cut off locks of hair or burned their arms in devotion. Months later the relic was returned and sealed in the crypt with gold and silver reliquaries, glass from the West and the finest tea utensils. In 1987, when the old pagoda was repaired, the crypt was opened and all of it was found where the Tang had left it.",
  "chinese-middle:rain": "Around 851 the poet Li Shangyin (李商隐), far from home in the mountains of Ba (巴), received a letter asking when he would come back. That night it rained, and he wrote one of the most loved short poems of the Tang (唐): You ask when I'll come home; I cannot say. The night rain in the Ba hills fills the autumn pools. When will we trim the candle by the west window together, and talk about this night of rain in the Ba hills? Ever since, rain at night has made Chinese readers think of someone far away.",
  "chinese-middle:drought": "In 1050 the harvest failed across the Wu (吴) region, and Fan Zhongyan (范仲淹), governor of Hangzhou (杭州), did something strange. Besides handing out grain, he encouraged boat races on the West Lake (西湖) and went out to watch them himself, urged the Buddhist temples to start building while labour was cheap, and rebuilt the granaries and offices. Critics said he was wasting money in a famine. He answered that the races and the building gave food and wages to tens of thousands of poor people who would otherwise have starved. The scientist Shen Kuo (沈括) recorded that Hangzhou came through that year better than any of its neighbours.",
  "chinese-middle:flood": "In the autumn of 1077 the Yellow River (黄河) broke its banks and the water surrounded Xuzhou (徐州), rising higher than the streets inside. The poet Su Shi (苏轼), governor there, stopped the rich from fleeing, saying that if they left everyone would panic. He moved into a hut on the city wall, went to the army camp in person to ask the soldiers to help, and led them building a long dike in the rain. For seventy days he did not go home. When the waters fell, he built the Yellow Tower (黄楼) on the wall, and the city held a feast there.",
  "chinese-middle:wind": "In the eighth month of 761 an autumn gale tore three layers of thatch from the roof of the poet Du Fu's (杜甫) cottage outside Chengdu (成都). Village boys ran off with the straw while he shouted after them until his voice gave out. That night rain poured through the roof onto his bedding, and his children slept in wet, cold quilts. In his Song of My Cottage Torn by the Autumn Wind (茅屋为秋风所破歌) he wished for a great mansion of ten thousand rooms to shelter all the poor scholars of the world, and said that if he saw it, he would gladly freeze alone.",
  "chinese-middle:fire": "Meng Yuanlao (孟元老), remembering the Song (宋) capital Kaifeng (开封), described how the city guarded itself against fire. Every few hundred paces stood a guard post with soldiers who patrolled at night, and on high ground rose brick fire-watch towers (望火楼) with lookouts day and night. Below them the fire brigades kept buckets, ropes, ladders, axes and wet hemp swatters. When a fire was seen, riders galloped to the army offices, and soldiers came running to put it out without asking the householders for a single coin. Hundreds of thousands lived under that watch.",
  "chinese-middle:thunder": "In the far south, on the Leizhou (雷州) peninsula, thunderstorms come almost every day in summer. The Tang (唐) people of Leizhou said that their first governor, Chen Wenyu (陈文玉), had been born from a great egg found by a hunter's dog after a thunderstorm, and that he had been the thunder god's son. When he died they built him a temple as the Lord of Thunder (雷祖). Tang writers reported that the people there kept drums for the thunder god and made offerings so that storms would spare their fields.",
  "chinese-middle:quake": "In the second month of 734 a great earthquake struck Qinzhou (秦州), around today's Tianshui (天水), on the road west from Chang'an (长安). The Tang (唐) histories say that houses and city walls fell and more than four thousand people, officials among them, were crushed. Emperor Xuanzong (唐玄宗) sent a minister to comfort the survivors: families who had lost members were given silk and grain, the dead were buried at public expense, and taxes were lifted for those who had lost everything. The town was rebuilt on new ground nearby.",
  "chinese-middle:plague": "The Tang (唐) physician Sun Simiao (孙思邈) refused offices from three emperors and spent his long life treating patients and gathering prescriptions. In 652 he finished his Prescriptions Worth a Thousand Gold (千金要方), written, he said, because a human life is worth more than a thousand pieces of gold. He taught that a great doctor must treat rich and poor, friend and stranger alike, go out at any hour through heat, cold, hunger and fatigue, and never be disgusted by a sick person. In time of epidemic, his book gave remedies anyone could make.",
  "chinese-middle:birth": "In Song (宋) cities, writers recall, a baby's third day and first month were celebrated with the bathing party (洗儿会). Relatives gathered around a basin of scented water, threw coins and fruit into it, stirred it with a hairpin, and the baby was washed and its hair shaved. When his son Su Dun (苏遁) was born in 1083, the poet Su Shi (苏轼), then in exile at Huangzhou (黄州), wrote a joking poem for the bath: everyone hopes their son will be clever; my cleverness has ruined my life. May my son be dull and simple, and live quietly to become a minister.",
  "chinese-middle:wedding": "Tang (唐) weddings had playful customs. When the groom arrived at the bride's house at dusk, the women of her family barred the gate and teased him, and he had to call out poems urging the bride to finish her makeup (催妆诗) before she would come out. On the way back, neighbours blocked the road (障车) and sang until they were given wine and food. The couple then entered a tent of blue cloth (青庐) set up in the courtyard, as the northern peoples did. The groom's friends sang and danced into the night.",
  "chinese-middle:death": "In 809 the Tang (唐) poet Yuan Zhen (元稹) lost his young wife Wei Cong (韦丛), who had married him when he was poor. In his three poems Elegies to Dispel Grief (遣悲怀) he remembered how she had searched her cases to find him clothes, pulled out her gold hairpin to buy him wine, and gathered fallen leaves for firewood. Now that he had a good salary, he wrote, he could only offer it to her at the memorial rites. Everyone knows this grief; but for a poor couple who shared everything, every memory hurts.",
  // ── Japanese, Heian: stories of the Heian court ──
  "japanese-early:showcase": "Gagaku (雅楽) came to Japan from Tang China (唐), Korea and beyond in the Nara (奈良) age, and in the Heian (平安) court of Kyoto (京都) it was sorted into two halves: tōgaku (唐楽) of the Left, from China and India, and komagaku (高麗楽) of the Right, from Korea. In 838 Fujiwara no Sadatoshi (藤原貞敏) sailed with an embassy to the Tang and came home with biwa (琵琶) pieces learned from a master in Yangzhou. Nobles played kangen (管絃) at parties by moonlight, the hichiriki (篳篥), ryūteki (龍笛) and shō (笙) over biwa and koto, and sang saibara (催馬楽) folk songs and rōei (朗詠) poems. The Tale of Genji (源氏物語) is full of such music, still played by the Imperial Household musicians today.",
  "japanese-early:hunt": "The Tales of Ise (伊勢物語) tell how Prince Koretaka (惟喬親王) went every spring to his villa at Minase (水無瀬) and rode out to hunt with falcons on the fields of Katano (交野). He always took the poet Ariwara no Narihira (在原業平) and other friends along. But they did little hunting. Instead they sat drinking under the cherry trees at the Nagisa (渚) villa and made poems. Narihira wrote: if there were no cherry blossoms in this world, how calm our hearts would be in spring. For Heian (平安) nobles, the hunt was a reason to go out into the blossoms.",
  "japanese-early:siege": "In 1062, in the far north of Japan, Minamoto no Yoriyoshi (源頼義) and his son Yoshiie (義家) besieged the Abe (安倍) clan in their stockade at Kuriyagawa (厨川柵). They filled the moat with brushwood and set it alight, and the fort burned. An old story says that earlier, at Koromogawa (衣川), Yoshiie had chased the Abe leader Sadato (貞任) and called after him a half poem: the threads of the robe are coming undone. Sadato turned in the saddle and finished it: the tangle of years of strain has worn them. Charmed, Yoshiie lowered his bow and let him go.",
  "japanese-early:triumph": "At the Heian (平安) court's great wrestling festival (相撲節会) in the seventh month, wrestlers gathered from every province were divided into teams of the Left and the Right. Victory was announced with music. When the Left won, musicians of the Left played and a dancer came out in the gilded mask of the warrior prince Ranryō-ō (蘭陵王), stamping in triumph; when the Right won, two dancers performed Nasori (納曽利), the paired dragons. Courtiers in silk watched from the pavilions of the palace, and the winning team's officers were given wine and gifts.",
  "japanese-early:gather": "On the first day of the rat in the New Year, Heian (平安) courtiers went out to the fields around Kyoto (京都) to pick young herbs (若菜摘み), which were cooked into a soup to keep away illness all year. Emperor Kōkō (光孝天皇), before he came to the throne, wrote a poem to go with such a gift: for you I went out to the spring fields to gather young greens, and on my sleeves the snow kept falling. It is one of the Hundred Poems by a Hundred Poets (百人一首). The custom lives on in the seven-herb rice porridge (七草粥) of the seventh day.",
  "japanese-early:build": "In 1053 Fujiwara no Yorimichi (藤原頼通) dedicated the Phoenix Hall (鳳凰堂) of the Byōdō-in (平等院) at Uji (宇治), a vision of Amida's Western Paradise reflected in a pond. Inside, the sculptor Jōchō (定朝) and his workshop carved the golden Amida (阿弥陀) and fifty-two small bodhisattvas riding on clouds (雲中供養菩薩) along the walls. Many of them play music: drums, flutes, a shō (笙), a biwa (琵琶), a harp and cymbals; others dance. Heian (平安) nobles believed the Buddha would come down with such music to fetch their souls at death.",
  "japanese-early:festival": "In the fourth month the Kamo (賀茂) festival filled Kyoto (京都), and everyone decorated their carriages and hats with hollyhock leaves (葵). The Tale of Genji (源氏物語) imagines it vividly: the streets so crowded that carriages fought for space to watch the procession. Lady Rokujō (六条御息所) had come in secret to glimpse Genji riding by, but the servants of his wife Aoi (葵の上) pushed her plain carriage to the back and broke its shafts. Humiliated, she watched him pass without a glance. Japanese still hold the Aoi festival (葵祭) every May.",
  "japanese-early:games": "Heian (平安) nobles played kemari (蹴鞠), keeping a deerskin ball in the air with their feet inside a court marked out by four trees: cherry, willow, maple and pine. There was no winning, only long, graceful rallies. Fujiwara no Narimichi (藤原成通), in the 12th century, was its great master: it is said he practised for seven thousand days and could kick the ball up while walking along a railing. Once, the story goes, the spirits of the ball appeared to him as three children with monkey faces and told him their names.",
  "japanese-early:ritual": "Each autumn at the Niiname-sai (新嘗祭), the harvest rite, the Heian (平安) emperor offered the new rice to the gods, and four or five young women chosen from noble families danced the Gosechi dance (五節舞) before him, their long sleeves turning five times. Legend said it began when heavenly maidens came down to dance for Emperor Tenmu (天武天皇) as he played the koto. The monk-poet Henjō (遍昭) wrote: winds of heaven, blow shut the pathway through the clouds, so that these maidens may stay a little longer. Today the dance is still performed at enthronements.",
  "japanese-early:flood": "The retired emperor Shirakawa (白河院), who ruled Japan from his cloister for more than forty years around 1100, is said to have named the only three things he could not control: the waters of the Kamo River (賀茂川), the fall of the dice, and the warrior monks of Mount Hiei (山法師). Every few years the Kamo flooded Kyoto (京都), sweeping away bridges and houses along its banks. A special office, the Bōkashi (防鴨河使), was set up to keep its levees, and the people of the eastern districts kept a watch on the sky in the rainy season.",
  "japanese-early:wind": "In the twelfth month of 934 the poet Ki no Tsurayuki (紀貫之) left his post in Tosa (土佐) to sail home to the capital, and wrote about it in the voice of a woman in the Tosa Diary (土佐日記). The wind decided everything. Day after day the boat sat in harbour while the wind blew against them; when it dropped they rowed out fearfully, watching for pirates. Once, in a storm, the captain told Tsurayuki to throw his precious mirror into the sea for the god, and the waves calmed. Through it all he mourned the little daughter who had died in Tosa.",
  "japanese-early:fire": "In the ninth month of 960 the imperial palace in Kyoto (京都) burned for the first time since the capital was founded in 794. The fire began near the Senyōden (宣陽殿), where treasures were kept, and spread through the halls in the night. Emperor Murakami (村上天皇), a fine musician, lost precious instruments and books; the courtiers feared above all for the sacred mirror. The story was told that it had flown out of the flames and hung itself in a cherry tree before the Shishinden (紫宸殿). The palace was rebuilt, but it would burn many more times.",
  "japanese-early:quake": "On the twenty-sixth day of the fifth month of 869, the Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku (日本三代実録) records, a great earthquake struck Mutsu (陸奥) in the north. People cried out and fell and could not stand; houses collapsed, and the castle town of Tagajō (多賀城) was wrecked. Then the sea rose like thunder and flooded the plain for many miles, and about a thousand people drowned. The Jōgan (貞観) tsunami was half forgotten until scientists found its sands in the soil, and the 2011 tsunami followed almost the same path.",
  "japanese-early:winter": "One snowy morning around 995, the Pillow Book (枕草子) recalls, the shutters of Empress Teishi's (定子) rooms had been closed against the cold, and the ladies sat around the brazier. The empress turned to Sei Shōnagon (清少納言) and asked: Shōnagon, how is the snow on Xianglu Peak (香炉峰)? Sei Shōnagon understood at once. She had the lattice raised and rolled up the blind, as in a poem by Bai Juyi (白居易), and the snow-covered garden shone in. The empress laughed. Winter, wrote Sei Shōnagon, is best in the early morning.",
  "japanese-early:plague": "In 869, when epidemics were again killing people in Kyoto (京都), sixty-six tall halberds were set up, one for each province of Japan, and the portable shrine of the god Gozu Tennō (牛頭天王) was carried to the Shinsen-en (神泉苑) garden to drive the angry spirits of disease away. So began the Gion goryōe (祇園御霊会), the Gion festival (祇園祭). Each July Kyoto still pulls its towering floats through the streets, and on them musicians play the gion-bayashi (祇園囃子) of flutes, bells and drums, the clanging, lilting sound of a prayer against plague.",
  "japanese-early:birth": "In 1008 Empress Shōshi (彰子), the daughter of Fujiwara no Michinaga (藤原道長), gave birth to a prince in her father's house. Murasaki Shikibu (紫式部), who served her, wrote it all down. Monks chanted in every room, mediums shrieked as the spirits were driven into them, and rice was scattered against demons. After the birth everything in the room was white: curtains, robes, screens. Each evening archers twanged their bowstrings (鳴弦) and scholars read aloud from the Chinese classics as the baby was bathed. Michinaga was so happy that he did not mind when the baby wet his robe.",
  "japanese-early:death": "In the twelfth month of 1027 Fujiwara no Michinaga (藤原道長), once the most powerful man at the Heian (平安) court, lay dying in the Amida Hall of his temple, the Hōjō-ji (法成寺) in Kyoto (京都). Facing west, he held in his hands five coloured threads tied to the hands of nine golden Amida (阿弥陀) statues, so that the Buddha would lead him to the Pure Land. Monks chanted the nembutsu (念仏) around him day and night. The man who had once written that the world was his, like the full moon, died praying to be carried away.",
  // ── Koreans, Silla–Goryeo: stories of Silla and Goryeo ──
  "koreans-early:showcase": "The Samguk Sagi (삼국사기) says King Gasil (가실왕) of Gaya (가야) had the gayageum (가야금) made in the 6th century, and that its master Ureuk (우륵) brought it to Silla (신라), where King Jinheung (진흥왕) had young men learn his twelve songs. Silla court music joined three strings and three bamboos (삼현삼죽): geomungo (거문고), gayageum and hyangbipa (향비파) with the great, middle and small flutes, the daegeum (대금), junggeum (중금) and sogeum (소금). Monks and hwarang (화랑) sang hyangga (향가) poems. Goryeo (고려) added Tang and Song banquet music (당악), and in 1116 Emperor Huizong (徽宗) sent the Song ritual music (대성아악) with bells and chimes. Goryeo songs (고려가요) such as Cheongsan byeolgok (청산별곡) are still sung today.",
  "koreans-early:hunt": "King Gongmin (공민왕) of Goryeo (고려), who reigned from 1351 to 1374, was a painter as well as a king. A small silk painting long kept as his work, the Great Hunt in the Tian Shan (천산대렵도), shows riders in fur hats galloping across a pale plain, one turning in the saddle to draw his bow behind him as the deer run. The style is that of the steppe horsemen whom Goryeo knew well from a century of close ties with the Yuan (원). Only fragments survive, but they still catch the speed of the chase.",
  "koreans-early:battle": "In 660 the Silla (신라) army met the Baekje (백제) general Gyebaek (계백) and his five thousand at Hwangsanbeol (황산벌) and was driven back four times. Then the general Kim Pum-il (김품일) sent out his sixteen-year-old son Gwanchang (관창), a hwarang (화랑). Gwanchang charged alone and was captured; Gyebaek, moved by his youth, sent him back alive. The boy drank water from a well, said he was ashamed to have returned, and charged again. This time his head came back tied to his saddle. The Silla soldiers, weeping with rage, charged together and won.",
  "koreans-early:siege": "In 1231 a Mongol (몽골) army under Saritai (살리타이) swept into Goryeo (고려) and surrounded the northern fortress of Guju (구주). Its commander Bak Seo (박서) held it for months. The Mongols built siege towers, dug tunnels and hurled stones and burning oil; the defenders poured molten iron into the tunnels, threw down boulders and doused the fires with mud and water. An old Mongol general who had seen many sieges said he had never seen a city stand like this one. Guju opened its gates only when the Goryeo court itself ordered Bak Seo to stop.",
  "koreans-early:gather": "In 828, the Samguk Sagi (삼국사기) records, the Silla (신라) envoy Kim Dae-ryeom (김대렴) came home from Tang China (당) with tea seeds, and King Heungdeok (흥덕왕) had them planted on Mount Jiri (지리산). Tea had been drunk in Silla before, but now it grew there. Monks of the mountain temples picked the young leaves in spring and offered tea before the Buddha; in Goryeo (고려) it became a court ceremony and a pleasure of poets. The wild tea bushes around Ssanggyesa (쌍계사) are said to come from those first seeds.",
  "koreans-early:build": "In 751 the Silla (신라) minister Kim Dae-seong (김대성), the Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) says, began two great works near Gyeongju (경주): the temple of Bulguksa (불국사) for his parents in this life, and the cave shrine of Seokguram (석굴암) on the mountain above for his parents in a former life. Masons cut granite blocks to fit together without mortar into a domed chamber, where a great white Buddha sits facing the East Sea, surrounded by carved guardians and disciples. Kim died before the work was finished; Silla completed it, and the first sunrise still reaches the Buddha's face.",
  "koreans-early:voyage": "Around 723 a young Silla (신라) monk named Hyecho (혜초) sailed from China to India to see the holy places of the Buddha. For four years he walked through the kingdoms of India, then over the Pamirs and through Persia and Central Asia, and came back by land to Chang'an (장안). He wrote a short travel diary, Memoir of a Pilgrimage to the Five Kingdoms of India (왕오천축국전). It was lost for a thousand years until in 1908 a fragment was found among the manuscripts of the Dunhuang (돈황) caves, with his short poems of homesickness for Silla.",
  "koreans-early:festival": "In the reign of King Yuri (유리왕), the Samguk Sagi (삼국사기) tells, the women of the Silla (신라) capital were divided into two teams, each led by a princess. From the middle of the seventh month they gathered every morning to spin and weave hemp, working late into the night. On the fifteenth day of the eighth month the cloth was measured, and the losing team served food and wine to the winners. Everyone sang and danced, and one of the losing women sang a sad, lovely song, hoeso, hoeso (회소곡). The festival was called gabae (가배), the root of Chuseok (추석).",
  "koreans-early:games": "In 1975, while draining the Anapji (안압지) pond of the Silla (신라) palace in Gyeongju (경주), archaeologists found a wooden die with fourteen faces, the juryeonggu (주령구). Each face carries a forfeit for a drinking game: drink three cups at once, sing a song by yourself, dance without music, recite a poem, let everyone flick your nose, laugh while being tickled, or keep still and silent. It was made for parties of nobles beside the pond under the moon. The original burned in an accident while being dried, but a copy keeps the game alive.",
  "koreans-early:ritual": "Each winter Goryeo (고려) held the Palgwanhoe (팔관회), a great festival in the capital Gaegyeong (개경) honouring the spirits of heaven, the mountains and the rivers along with the Buddha. Lanterns lit the palace courtyard, and on a decorated stage dancers and musicians performed the old Silla (신라) dances. Officials came from every province with gifts, and merchants from Song China (송), Japan, the Jurchen (여진) lands and even Arabia were received at court. For Goryeo it was a rite of the whole land: the king, the gods and the people feasting together.",
  "koreans-early:rain": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells that in 682 a small mountain floated toward the Silla (신라) shore. On it grew a bamboo that split in two by day and joined into one at night. King Sinmun (신문왕) went to see it, and a dragon told him that if he made a flute from the bamboo, the land would know peace. He did. When the flute was played, enemies withdrew and sickness passed; in drought it brought rain, and in floods it cleared the sky; the winds dropped and the waves grew calm. The flute was named Manpasikjeok (만파식적), the flute that calms ten thousand waves.",
  "koreans-early:drought": "Imagined: in a Goryeo (고려) village in a dry summer, the rice seedlings yellowed in the cracked paddies. The village headman sent word to the local magistrate, who led the elders up to the mountain shrine at dawn to offer a pig and wine, while a monk from the temple below chanted sutras for rain. The children were sent to splash water at a straw dragon by the dried stream, laughing in spite of their parents' worry. For many evenings the families watched the clouds come and go over the hills, until the night the rain finally drummed on the thatch.",
  "koreans-early:flood": "Imagined: in a summer of long rains in the Silla (신라) capital of Gyeongju (경주), the Hyeongsan (형산강) and the streams that ran past the palace overflowed together. Water swept through the lanes of tile-roofed houses, and families climbed onto the walls of the temple courtyards. When it fell back, the farmers found their fields covered in mud and sand. The king opened the granaries, and monks and villagers worked side by side to rebuild the banks. The chronicles of Silla record such floods often, a line or two each, with the number of houses lost.",
  "koreans-early:wind": "In 1281 Goryeo (고려) carpenters and sailors, ordered by the Yuan (원) court, built hundreds of ships for a second invasion of Japan, and the Goryeo general Kim Bang-gyeong (김방경) sailed with the eastern fleet from Happo (합포). Off the Japanese coast, in the eighth month, a typhoon struck. The ships, built in haste, broke apart and sank by the hundred, and many thousands of Goryeo soldiers and sailors drowned. Kim Bang-gyeong came home with the survivors. In Goryeo ports, the widows of the oarsmen never forgot the storm the Japanese called the divine wind.",
  "koreans-early:fire": "In 1232 Mongol (몽골) raiders burned the temple of Buinsa (부인사) near Daegu (대구), and with it the woodblocks of the first Goryeo (고려) Tripitaka, the Buddhist canon carved two centuries earlier. The Goryeo court, sheltering on Ganghwa Island (강화도), decided to carve it all again as a prayer for protection. From 1236 to 1251 monks and craftsmen soaked, boiled and dried birch and cherry wood and cut more than eighty thousand blocks, each character carved with a bow, it is said. The Tripitaka Koreana (팔만대장경) is still kept at Haeinsa (해인사).",
  "koreans-early:thunder": "The wooden pagoda of Hwangnyongsa (황룡사) in the Silla (신라) capital of Gyeongju (경주), built in 645 by the Baekje master craftsman Abiji (아비지), rose nine storeys, some eighty metres high, the tallest building in Korea. Because it stood so high above the plain, the chronicles record that it was struck by lightning again and again over the centuries, and each time Silla and then Goryeo (고려) repaired it. The people of Gyeongju watched the storms roll over it and felt that the nine storeys were guarding the land. It stood for nearly six hundred years.",
  "koreans-early:winter": "Around 1241 the Goryeo (고려) poet Yi Gyu-bo (이규보) wrote six poems on the vegetables in his garden. Of the radish he wrote that, pickled in brine and soy, it is good through the long winter. It is one of the oldest records of what would become kimchi (김치). In his day there was no red pepper yet; Goryeo families salted radishes, cucumbers and greens in jars, buried them in the cold earth, and dug them out on snowy days when nothing grew. Yi Gyu-bo, who wrote thousands of poems, found as much to say about turnips as about the moon.",
  "koreans-early:plague": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells of Cheoyong (처용), a son of the Dragon King of the East Sea who came to serve King Heongang (헌강왕) of Silla (신라) in the 870s. One night he came home and saw two pairs of legs in his bed: the spirit of plague had taken the form of a man and lay with his wife. Cheoyong did not rage. He sang and danced, and went out. The spirit was so moved that it knelt and swore never to enter any house that bore Cheoyong's picture. Koreans hung his face on their gates, and the Cheoyong dance (처용무) is still performed.",
  "koreans-early:birth": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells how Silla (신라) began in 57 BC. The chiefs of the six villages of Gyeongju (경주) gathered on a hill to pray for a king. Below, by a well called Najeong (나정), lightning flashed and a white horse knelt. When they went down, the horse cried and flew up to heaven, leaving a great red egg. From it hatched a shining boy. When they bathed him in the Dongcheon stream, birds and beasts danced, and the sun and moon grew brighter. They named him Bak Hyeokgeose (박혁거세), the one who brightens the world.",
  "koreans-early:wedding": "The Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) tells that a poor young man of Baekje (백제) who dug yams for a living, Seodong (서동), heard of the beauty of Princess Seonhwa (선화공주) of Silla (신라). He went to Gyeongju (경주), gave yams to the children, and taught them a little song (서동요): Princess Seonhwa secretly married, goes at night to embrace Seodong. The children sang it in every street until the king heard and sent his daughter away in disgrace. Seodong met her on the road, and she married him after all. He is said to have become King Mu (무왕) of Baekje.",
  "koreans-early:death": "In the 8th century the Silla (신라) monk Wolmyeong (월명사), a famous player of the flute, lost his younger sister. At her memorial rite he sang a hyangga (향가), the Song for a Dead Sister (제망매가): the path of life and death is here, and I am afraid; you went without even saying you were going. Like leaves blown by an early autumn wind, scattered from one branch, we do not know where we go. Then, the Samguk Yusa (삼국유사) says, a sudden wind blew the paper money for the dead away to the west, toward the Pure Land where he prayed to meet her again.",
});
